            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-86409",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "curl: curl: stale SigV4 headers sent unintended"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A flaw was found in curl. When an application attempts to disable Amazon Web Services Signature Version 4 (AWS SigV4) authentication on a reused connection handle, curl fails to clear the setting as expected. If the application subsequently directs a request to an untrusted or attacker-controlled server using the same handle, curl unintentionally transmits the signed authorization header. This flaw allows a remote attacker to obtain access key identifiers and request signatures, leading to information disclosure."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-86409",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-86409\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: libcurl4t64\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-86409](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-86409)\nA flaw was found in curl. When an application attempts to disable Amazon Web Services Signature Version 4 (AWS SigV4) authentication on a reused connection handle, curl fails to clear the setting as expected. If the application subsequently directs a request to an untrusted or attacker-controlled server using the same handle, curl unintentionally transmits the signed authorization header. This flaw allows a remote attacker to obtain access key identifiers and request signatures, leading to information disclosure.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-86409**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|libcurl4t64||[CVE-2026-86409](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-86409)|\n\nA flaw was found in curl. When an application attempts to disable Amazon Web Services Signature Version 4 (AWS SigV4) authentication on a reused connection handle, curl fails to clear the setting as expected. If the application subsequently directs a request to an untrusted or attacker-controlled server using the same handle, curl unintentionally transmits the signed authorization header. This flaw allows a remote attacker to obtain access key identifiers and request signatures, leading to information disclosure."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
