            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-93824",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: tls: reject the combination of TLS and sockmap"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ntls: reject the combination of TLS and sockmap\n\nTLS and sockmap (BPF psock) integration hides a lot of latent bugs.\nBugs which may be more or less relevant for real users but they\nare definitely exploitable.\n\nWe could not find anyone actively using this integration so let's\nreject this config. Adding a TLS socket to a sockmap was already\nrejected by sk_psock_init() through the inet_csk_has_ulp() check.\nWe need to reject the attempts to configure the TLS keys (rather\nthan adding the ULP itself) because checking prior to the ULP\ninstallation is tricky without risking a race with sockmap getting\nadded in parallel (sockmap does not hold the socket lock).\n\nThis patch is a minimal rejection of the feature. Subsequent patch\nin the series will do a light dead code removal. Full cleanup would\nrequire a major rewrite of the Tx path, we don't need skmsg any more."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-93824",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-93824\nSeverity: HIGH\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-93824](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-93824)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ntls: reject the combination of TLS and sockmap\n\nTLS and sockmap (BPF psock) integration hides a lot of latent bugs.\nBugs which may be more or less relevant for real users but they\nare definitely exploitable.\n\nWe could not find anyone actively using this integration so let's\nreject this config. Adding a TLS socket to a sockmap was already\nrejected by sk_psock_init() through the inet_csk_has_ulp() check.\nWe need to reject the attempts to configure the TLS keys (rather\nthan adding the ULP itself) because checking prior to the ULP\ninstallation is tricky without risking a race with sockmap getting\nadded in parallel (sockmap does not hold the socket lock).\n\nThis patch is a minimal rejection of the feature. Subsequent patch\nin the series will do a light dead code removal. Full cleanup would\nrequire a major rewrite of the Tx path, we don't need skmsg any more.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-93824**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|HIGH|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-93824](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-93824)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ntls: reject the combination of TLS and sockmap\n\nTLS and sockmap (BPF psock) integration hides a lot of latent bugs.\nBugs which may be more or less relevant for real users but they\nare definitely exploitable.\n\nWe could not find anyone actively using this integration so let's\nreject this config. Adding a TLS socket to a sockmap was already\nrejected by sk_psock_init() through the inet_csk_has_ulp() check.\nWe need to reject the attempts to configure the TLS keys (rather\nthan adding the ULP itself) because checking prior to the ULP\ninstallation is tricky without risking a race with sockmap getting\nadded in parallel (sockmap does not hold the socket lock).\n\nThis patch is a minimal rejection of the feature. Subsequent patch\nin the series will do a light dead code removal. Full cleanup would\nrequire a major rewrite of the Tx path, we don't need skmsg any more."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "HIGH"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-93828",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: exfat: fix handling of damaged volume in exfat_create_upcase_table()"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nexfat: fix handling of damaged volume in exfat_create_upcase_table()\n\nWhen the size of the upcase table is set to zero in the dentry for any\nreason(e.g. corrupted media or misbehaving device), an integer overflow\ncauses the module to loop indefinitely.\n\nIf the size of the upcase table is read zero, do not attempt to load the\ntable. Instead, fallback to loading the default upcase table. If the\nsize of the upcase table is zero or no upcase table is found, raise\nexfat_fs_error() to mark the volume read-only."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-93828",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-93828\nSeverity: HIGH\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-93828](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-93828)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nexfat: fix handling of damaged volume in exfat_create_upcase_table()\n\nWhen the size of the upcase table is set to zero in the dentry for any\nreason(e.g. corrupted media or misbehaving device), an integer overflow\ncauses the module to loop indefinitely.\n\nIf the size of the upcase table is read zero, do not attempt to load the\ntable. Instead, fallback to loading the default upcase table. If the\nsize of the upcase table is zero or no upcase table is found, raise\nexfat_fs_error() to mark the volume read-only.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-93828**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|HIGH|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-93828](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-93828)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nexfat: fix handling of damaged volume in exfat_create_upcase_table()\n\nWhen the size of the upcase table is set to zero in the dentry for any\nreason(e.g. corrupted media or misbehaving device), an integer overflow\ncauses the module to loop indefinitely.\n\nIf the size of the upcase table is read zero, do not attempt to load the\ntable. Instead, fallback to loading the default upcase table. If the\nsize of the upcase table is zero or no upcase table is found, raise\nexfat_fs_error() to mark the volume read-only."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "HIGH"
                ]
              }
