            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-98041",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: bpf: Don't predict JMP32 pointer vs zero comparisons"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nbpf: Don't predict JMP32 pointer vs zero comparisons\n\nConsider the following program:\n\n        r1 = map_value;      /* low 32 bits are zero at runtime */\n        r6 = 0xdead000000000000;\n        if w1 != 0 goto l1;\n    l0: r1 += r6;\n        r2 = *(u64 *)(r1 + 0);\n        exit;\n    l1: r6 = 0;\n        goto l0;\n\nAt the moment is_branch_taken() reports the jump as always taken,\nbecause it does not distinguish between BPF_JMP and BPF_JMP32\ncomparisons when processing 'if w1 != 0 ...'."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98041",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-98041\nSeverity: HIGH\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-98041](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98041)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nbpf: Don't predict JMP32 pointer vs zero comparisons\n\nConsider the following program:\n\n        r1 = map_value;      /* low 32 bits are zero at runtime */\n        r6 = 0xdead000000000000;\n        if w1 != 0 goto l1;\n    l0: r1 += r6;\n        r2 = *(u64 *)(r1 + 0);\n        exit;\n    l1: r6 = 0;\n        goto l0;\n\nAt the moment is_branch_taken() reports the jump as always taken,\nbecause it does not distinguish between BPF_JMP and BPF_JMP32\ncomparisons when processing 'if w1 != 0 ...'.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-98041**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|HIGH|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-98041](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98041)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nbpf: Don't predict JMP32 pointer vs zero comparisons\n\nConsider the following program:\n\n        r1 = map_value;      /* low 32 bits are zero at runtime */\n        r6 = 0xdead000000000000;\n        if w1 != 0 goto l1;\n    l0: r1 += r6;\n        r2 = *(u64 *)(r1 + 0);\n        exit;\n    l1: r6 = 0;\n        goto l0;\n\nAt the moment is_branch_taken() reports the jump as always taken,\nbecause it does not distinguish between BPF_JMP and BPF_JMP32\ncomparisons when processing 'if w1 != 0 ...'."
              },
