            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-72321",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: ipv4: igmp: Fix potential memory leaks in igmp_mod_timer() and igmp_stop_timer()"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nipv4: igmp: Fix potential memory leaks in igmp_mod_timer() and igmp_stop_timer()\n\nWhen a timer is deleted and not re-armed in igmp_mod_timer(), or stopped\nin igmp_stop_timer(), the code currently decrements the reference counter\nof the multicast list entry @im using refcount_dec(\u0026im-\u003erefcnt).\n\nHowever, both functions can be called from the RCU reader path:\n- igmp_mod_timer() via igmp_heard_query() -\u003e for_each_pmc_rcu()\n- igmp_stop_timer() via igmp_rcv() -\u003e igmp_heard_report()\n\nIf the group im was concurrently removed from the list by ip_mc_dec_group(),\nits reference count might have already been decremented to 1.\n\nIn this case, timer_delete() succeeds, and refcount_dec() decrements\nthe refcount from 1 to 0. Since refcount_dec() does not free the object\nwhen it hits 0 (unlike ip_ma_put()), the im structure is leaked.\n\nFix this by using ip_ma_put(im) instead of refcount_dec(\u0026im-\u003erefcnt),\nand deferring the put until after the spinlock is released."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-72321",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-72321\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-72321](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-72321)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nipv4: igmp: Fix potential memory leaks in igmp_mod_timer() and igmp_stop_timer()\n\nWhen a timer is deleted and not re-armed in igmp_mod_timer(), or stopped\nin igmp_stop_timer(), the code currently decrements the reference counter\nof the multicast list entry @im using refcount_dec(\u0026im-\u003erefcnt).\n\nHowever, both functions can be called from the RCU reader path:\n- igmp_mod_timer() via igmp_heard_query() -\u003e for_each_pmc_rcu()\n- igmp_stop_timer() via igmp_rcv() -\u003e igmp_heard_report()\n\nIf the group im was concurrently removed from the list by ip_mc_dec_group(),\nits reference count might have already been decremented to 1.\n\nIn this case, timer_delete() succeeds, and refcount_dec() decrements\nthe refcount from 1 to 0. Since refcount_dec() does not free the object\nwhen it hits 0 (unlike ip_ma_put()), the im structure is leaked.\n\nFix this by using ip_ma_put(im) instead of refcount_dec(\u0026im-\u003erefcnt),\nand deferring the put until after the spinlock is released.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-72321**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-72321](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-72321)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nipv4: igmp: Fix potential memory leaks in igmp_mod_timer() and igmp_stop_timer()\n\nWhen a timer is deleted and not re-armed in igmp_mod_timer(), or stopped\nin igmp_stop_timer(), the code currently decrements the reference counter\nof the multicast list entry @im using refcount_dec(\u0026im-\u003erefcnt).\n\nHowever, both functions can be called from the RCU reader path:\n- igmp_mod_timer() via igmp_heard_query() -\u003e for_each_pmc_rcu()\n- igmp_stop_timer() via igmp_rcv() -\u003e igmp_heard_report()\n\nIf the group im was concurrently removed from the list by ip_mc_dec_group(),\nits reference count might have already been decremented to 1.\n\nIn this case, timer_delete() succeeds, and refcount_dec() decrements\nthe refcount from 1 to 0. Since refcount_dec() does not free the object\nwhen it hits 0 (unlike ip_ma_put()), the im structure is leaked.\n\nFix this by using ip_ma_put(im) instead of refcount_dec(\u0026im-\u003erefcnt),\nand deferring the put until after the spinlock is released."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-72334",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: Bluetooth: ISO: fix malformed ISO_END/CONT handling"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nBluetooth: ISO: fix malformed ISO_END/CONT handling\n\nCore specification (Part C vol 4 sec 5.4.5) does not exclude empty\nISO_CONT, ISO_END packets.  We currently reject them if they are last.\n\nIf controller sends malformed sequence\n\n    ISO_START -\u003e rx_len = 4, ISO_CONT skb-\u003elen 4, ISO_START\n\nthat ends payload in ISO_CONT, we leak conn-\u003erx_skb. If controller sends\ntoo long ISO_END, we panic on skb_put. If controller sends too short\nISO_END we accept it.\n\nFix by marking unfinished ISO_START via conn-\u003erx_skb != NULL.  Check\nskb-\u003elen properly before skb_put.  Combine the ISO_CONT/END code paths\nas they require the same initial checks. Reject too short ISO_END\npackets."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-72334",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-72334\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-72334](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-72334)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nBluetooth: ISO: fix malformed ISO_END/CONT handling\n\nCore specification (Part C vol 4 sec 5.4.5) does not exclude empty\nISO_CONT, ISO_END packets.  We currently reject them if they are last.\n\nIf controller sends malformed sequence\n\n    ISO_START -\u003e rx_len = 4, ISO_CONT skb-\u003elen 4, ISO_START\n\nthat ends payload in ISO_CONT, we leak conn-\u003erx_skb. If controller sends\ntoo long ISO_END, we panic on skb_put. If controller sends too short\nISO_END we accept it.\n\nFix by marking unfinished ISO_START via conn-\u003erx_skb != NULL.  Check\nskb-\u003elen properly before skb_put.  Combine the ISO_CONT/END code paths\nas they require the same initial checks. Reject too short ISO_END\npackets.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-72334**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-72334](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-72334)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nBluetooth: ISO: fix malformed ISO_END/CONT handling\n\nCore specification (Part C vol 4 sec 5.4.5) does not exclude empty\nISO_CONT, ISO_END packets.  We currently reject them if they are last.\n\nIf controller sends malformed sequence\n\n    ISO_START -\u003e rx_len = 4, ISO_CONT skb-\u003elen 4, ISO_START\n\nthat ends payload in ISO_CONT, we leak conn-\u003erx_skb. If controller sends\ntoo long ISO_END, we panic on skb_put. If controller sends too short\nISO_END we accept it.\n\nFix by marking unfinished ISO_START via conn-\u003erx_skb != NULL.  Check\nskb-\u003elen properly before skb_put.  Combine the ISO_CONT/END code paths\nas they require the same initial checks. Reject too short ISO_END\npackets."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-72345",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: net/mlx5: LAG, Fix off-by-one in single-FDB error rollback"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet/mlx5: LAG, Fix off-by-one in single-FDB error rollback\n\nOn failure at index i, the reverse cleanup loop in\nmlx5_lag_create_single_fdb() starts from i, so the failed index\nitself is rolled back. That can operate on uninitialized state or\ndouble-tear-down a rule the add_one path already self-rolled-back.\n\nStart the rollback from i - 1 so only successfully-installed entries\nare undone."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-72345",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-72345\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-72345](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-72345)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet/mlx5: LAG, Fix off-by-one in single-FDB error rollback\n\nOn failure at index i, the reverse cleanup loop in\nmlx5_lag_create_single_fdb() starts from i, so the failed index\nitself is rolled back. That can operate on uninitialized state or\ndouble-tear-down a rule the add_one path already self-rolled-back.\n\nStart the rollback from i - 1 so only successfully-installed entries\nare undone.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-72345**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-72345](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-72345)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet/mlx5: LAG, Fix off-by-one in single-FDB error rollback\n\nOn failure at index i, the reverse cleanup loop in\nmlx5_lag_create_single_fdb() starts from i, so the failed index\nitself is rolled back. That can operate on uninitialized state or\ndouble-tear-down a rule the add_one path already self-rolled-back.\n\nStart the rollback from i - 1 so only successfully-installed entries\nare undone."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
