            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-89896",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: media: cedrus: fix memory leak in cedrus_init_ctrls()"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmedia: cedrus: fix memory leak in cedrus_init_ctrls()\n\nIn cedrus_init_ctrls(), the V4L2 control handler is initialized before\nallocating memory for ctx-\u003ectrls. If this allocation fails, the function\nreturns -ENOMEM without freeing the previously allocated handler\nresources, leading to a memory leak.\n\nFix this by calling v4l2_ctrl_handler_free() on the ctx-\u003ectrls allocation\nfailure path.\n\nThe bug was first flagged by an experimental analysis tool we are\ndeveloping for kernel memory-management bugs while analyzing\nv6.13-rc1. The tool is still under development and is not yet publicly\navailable. Manual inspection confirms that the bug is still\npresent in v7.1.1.\n\nAn x86_64 allyesconfig build showed no new warnings. As we do not have an\nAllwinner SoC or board with a Cedrus VPU available to test with, no\nruntime testing was able to be performed."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-89896",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-89896\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-89896](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-89896)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmedia: cedrus: fix memory leak in cedrus_init_ctrls()\n\nIn cedrus_init_ctrls(), the V4L2 control handler is initialized before\nallocating memory for ctx-\u003ectrls. If this allocation fails, the function\nreturns -ENOMEM without freeing the previously allocated handler\nresources, leading to a memory leak.\n\nFix this by calling v4l2_ctrl_handler_free() on the ctx-\u003ectrls allocation\nfailure path.\n\nThe bug was first flagged by an experimental analysis tool we are\ndeveloping for kernel memory-management bugs while analyzing\nv6.13-rc1. The tool is still under development and is not yet publicly\navailable. Manual inspection confirms that the bug is still\npresent in v7.1.1.\n\nAn x86_64 allyesconfig build showed no new warnings. As we do not have an\nAllwinner SoC or board with a Cedrus VPU available to test with, no\nruntime testing was able to be performed.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-89896**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-89896](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-89896)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmedia: cedrus: fix memory leak in cedrus_init_ctrls()\n\nIn cedrus_init_ctrls(), the V4L2 control handler is initialized before\nallocating memory for ctx-\u003ectrls. If this allocation fails, the function\nreturns -ENOMEM without freeing the previously allocated handler\nresources, leading to a memory leak.\n\nFix this by calling v4l2_ctrl_handler_free() on the ctx-\u003ectrls allocation\nfailure path.\n\nThe bug was first flagged by an experimental analysis tool we are\ndeveloping for kernel memory-management bugs while analyzing\nv6.13-rc1. The tool is still under development and is not yet publicly\navailable. Manual inspection confirms that the bug is still\npresent in v7.1.1.\n\nAn x86_64 allyesconfig build showed no new warnings. As we do not have an\nAllwinner SoC or board with a Cedrus VPU available to test with, no\nruntime testing was able to be performed."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
