            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-97523",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: mptcp: close race between scheduler and state change"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmptcp: close race between scheduler and state change\n\nThe mptcp scheduler may race with subflow sockets state change: data\ntransmission on the selected socket may fail and a later release could\ntry to use mss_now reset to 0 for a divide operation.\n\nAddress the issue by explicitly checking for the critical scenario."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97523",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-97523\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-97523](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97523)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmptcp: close race between scheduler and state change\n\nThe mptcp scheduler may race with subflow sockets state change: data\ntransmission on the selected socket may fail and a later release could\ntry to use mss_now reset to 0 for a divide operation.\n\nAddress the issue by explicitly checking for the critical scenario.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-97523**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-97523](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97523)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmptcp: close race between scheduler and state change\n\nThe mptcp scheduler may race with subflow sockets state change: data\ntransmission on the selected socket may fail and a later release could\ntry to use mss_now reset to 0 for a divide operation.\n\nAddress the issue by explicitly checking for the critical scenario."
              },
