            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-97907",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: Bluetooth: btrtl: Don't leak return code when parsing firmware format v2"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nBluetooth: btrtl: Don't leak return code when parsing firmware format v2\n\nWhen key_id from chip is zero, rtlbt_parse_firmware_v2() intentionally\nignores all security headers. However, the implementation simply breaks\nfrom a switch statement and leaks uninitialized return code `rc' (if the\nfirst section is a security one) or the previous section's `rc'.\n\nFix it by really skipping a loop with `continue'. For consistency and\nreadability, also do the same for the default case."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97907",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-97907\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-97907](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97907)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nBluetooth: btrtl: Don't leak return code when parsing firmware format v2\n\nWhen key_id from chip is zero, rtlbt_parse_firmware_v2() intentionally\nignores all security headers. However, the implementation simply breaks\nfrom a switch statement and leaks uninitialized return code `rc' (if the\nfirst section is a security one) or the previous section's `rc'.\n\nFix it by really skipping a loop with `continue'. For consistency and\nreadability, also do the same for the default case.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-97907**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-97907](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97907)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nBluetooth: btrtl: Don't leak return code when parsing firmware format v2\n\nWhen key_id from chip is zero, rtlbt_parse_firmware_v2() intentionally\nignores all security headers. However, the implementation simply breaks\nfrom a switch statement and leaks uninitialized return code `rc' (if the\nfirst section is a security one) or the previous section's `rc'.\n\nFix it by really skipping a loop with `continue'. For consistency and\nreadability, also do the same for the default case."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-97909",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: ASoC: sti: initialize IRQ lock before requesting IRQ"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nASoC: sti: initialize IRQ lock before requesting IRQ\n\nuni_reader_init() registers the shared IRQ before initializing\nreader-\u003eirq_lock. A pending interrupt can invoke the handler while the\nlock is still uninitialized.\n\nInitialize the lock before registering the IRQ so the interrupt path\nalways sees valid lock state."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97909",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-97909\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-97909](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97909)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nASoC: sti: initialize IRQ lock before requesting IRQ\n\nuni_reader_init() registers the shared IRQ before initializing\nreader-\u003eirq_lock. A pending interrupt can invoke the handler while the\nlock is still uninitialized.\n\nInitialize the lock before registering the IRQ so the interrupt path\nalways sees valid lock state.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-97909**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-97909](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97909)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nASoC: sti: initialize IRQ lock before requesting IRQ\n\nuni_reader_init() registers the shared IRQ before initializing\nreader-\u003eirq_lock. A pending interrupt can invoke the handler while the\nlock is still uninitialized.\n\nInitialize the lock before registering the IRQ so the interrupt path\nalways sees valid lock state."
              },
              "properties": {
