            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-97979",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: ice: add missing xa_destroy for sched_node_ids"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nice: add missing xa_destroy for sched_node_ids\n\nCommit 16dfa49406bc (\"ice: Introduce new parameters in ice_sched_node\")\nadded a sched_node_ids xarray to the port info structure, but never called\nxa_destroy on it.\n\nSince xarrays can allocate internal memory, this can result in a memory\nleak even if every element in the xarray has been removed.\n\nThe xarray is currently embedded in the port_info structure. This appears\nto have been done because its use is within functions that take the\nport_info as a primary argument.\n\nHowever, this complicates managing the lifecycle of the field. The\nport_info structure is allocated in ice_init_hw() using devm, and it is\nnot released until the devm cleanup when the driver is unloaded.\n\nThe ice_init_hw() function is called in many places, including devlink\nreload, and possibly during DDP load after updating the Tx scheduler\nlayout.\n\nAdding a call of xa_destroy to the ice_deinit_hw() causes Sashiko to raise\nmultiple concerns due to potential ordering issues and possible ways that\nport_info could be a dangling reference.\n\nTo handle this, move the sched_node_ids out of port_info and into the hw\nstructure. All users of the array already have a pointer to hw anyways, and\nthere is only one sched_node_ids per adapter. While here, remove the overly\nverbose comment explaining the nature of the sched_node_ids xarray.\n\nAdd the missing xa_destroy to the cleanup path and to ice_deinit_hw(),\nensuring that we properly release the xarray memory.\n\nThis was caught by Sashiko during development of unrelated code."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97979",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-97979\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-97979](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97979)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nice: add missing xa_destroy for sched_node_ids\n\nCommit 16dfa49406bc (\"ice: Introduce new parameters in ice_sched_node\")\nadded a sched_node_ids xarray to the port info structure, but never called\nxa_destroy on it.\n\nSince xarrays can allocate internal memory, this can result in a memory\nleak even if every element in the xarray has been removed.\n\nThe xarray is currently embedded in the port_info structure. This appears\nto have been done because its use is within functions that take the\nport_info as a primary argument.\n\nHowever, this complicates managing the lifecycle of the field. The\nport_info structure is allocated in ice_init_hw() using devm, and it is\nnot released until the devm cleanup when the driver is unloaded.\n\nThe ice_init_hw() function is called in many places, including devlink\nreload, and possibly during DDP load after updating the Tx scheduler\nlayout.\n\nAdding a call of xa_destroy to the ice_deinit_hw() causes Sashiko to raise\nmultiple concerns due to potential ordering issues and possible ways that\nport_info could be a dangling reference.\n\nTo handle this, move the sched_node_ids out of port_info and into the hw\nstructure. All users of the array already have a pointer to hw anyways, and\nthere is only one sched_node_ids per adapter. While here, remove the overly\nverbose comment explaining the nature of the sched_node_ids xarray.\n\nAdd the missing xa_destroy to the cleanup path and to ice_deinit_hw(),\nensuring that we properly release the xarray memory.\n\nThis was caught by Sashiko during development of unrelated code.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-97979**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-97979](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97979)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nice: add missing xa_destroy for sched_node_ids\n\nCommit 16dfa49406bc (\"ice: Introduce new parameters in ice_sched_node\")\nadded a sched_node_ids xarray to the port info structure, but never called\nxa_destroy on it.\n\nSince xarrays can allocate internal memory, this can result in a memory\nleak even if every element in the xarray has been removed.\n\nThe xarray is currently embedded in the port_info structure. This appears\nto have been done because its use is within functions that take the\nport_info as a primary argument.\n\nHowever, this complicates managing the lifecycle of the field. The\nport_info structure is allocated in ice_init_hw() using devm, and it is\nnot released until the devm cleanup when the driver is unloaded.\n\nThe ice_init_hw() function is called in many places, including devlink\nreload, and possibly during DDP load after updating the Tx scheduler\nlayout.\n\nAdding a call of xa_destroy to the ice_deinit_hw() causes Sashiko to raise\nmultiple concerns due to potential ordering issues and possible ways that\nport_info could be a dangling reference.\n\nTo handle this, move the sched_node_ids out of port_info and into the hw\nstructure. All users of the array already have a pointer to hw anyways, and\nthere is only one sched_node_ids per adapter. While here, remove the overly\nverbose comment explaining the nature of the sched_node_ids xarray.\n\nAdd the missing xa_destroy to the cleanup path and to ice_deinit_hw(),\nensuring that we properly release the xarray memory.\n\nThis was caught by Sashiko during development of unrelated code."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-97981",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: net: ethernet: cortina: Count dropped frames as NAPI work"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet: ethernet: cortina: Count dropped frames as NAPI work\n\nThe RX loop only consumes budget when it successfully delivers a frame.\nError paths keep consuming descriptors without reducing the budget, so a\nstream of bad frames can process the entire receive ring in one poll.\n\nMove the budget accounting to a common end-of-frame path. This counts\neach completed frame as NAPI work whether it was delivered or dropped,\nmatching the behavior of the vendor driver."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97981",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-97981\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-97981](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97981)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet: ethernet: cortina: Count dropped frames as NAPI work\n\nThe RX loop only consumes budget when it successfully delivers a frame.\nError paths keep consuming descriptors without reducing the budget, so a\nstream of bad frames can process the entire receive ring in one poll.\n\nMove the budget accounting to a common end-of-frame path. This counts\neach completed frame as NAPI work whether it was delivered or dropped,\nmatching the behavior of the vendor driver.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-97981**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-97981](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97981)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet: ethernet: cortina: Count dropped frames as NAPI work\n\nThe RX loop only consumes budget when it successfully delivers a frame.\nError paths keep consuming descriptors without reducing the budget, so a\nstream of bad frames can process the entire receive ring in one poll.\n\nMove the budget accounting to a common end-of-frame path. This counts\neach completed frame as NAPI work whether it was delivered or dropped,\nmatching the behavior of the vendor driver."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-97982",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: net: ethernet: cortina: Fix budget accounting"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet: ethernet: cortina: Fix budget accounting\n\nThe gmac_rx() function returns the remaining NAPI budget, but its\ncaller treats the return value as the number of packets received. An\nidle poll therefore reports a full budget and remains scheduled.\n\nReturn the number of received packets instead. Preserve the existing\nfree queue refill accounting by adding that count directly; continuing\nto subtract it from the budget would invert the refill behavior."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97982",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-97982\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-97982](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97982)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet: ethernet: cortina: Fix budget accounting\n\nThe gmac_rx() function returns the remaining NAPI budget, but its\ncaller treats the return value as the number of packets received. An\nidle poll therefore reports a full budget and remains scheduled.\n\nReturn the number of received packets instead. Preserve the existing\nfree queue refill accounting by adding that count directly; continuing\nto subtract it from the budget would invert the refill behavior.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-97982**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-97982](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97982)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet: ethernet: cortina: Fix budget accounting\n\nThe gmac_rx() function returns the remaining NAPI budget, but its\ncaller treats the return value as the number of packets received. An\nidle poll therefore reports a full budget and remains scheduled.\n\nReturn the number of received packets instead. Preserve the existing\nfree queue refill accounting by adding that count directly; continuing\nto subtract it from the budget would invert the refill behavior."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-97984",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: net: ipv6: Fix UDP length overflow with PMTU discover and big MTU"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet: ipv6: Fix UDP length overflow with PMTU discover and big MTU\n\nThis commit bounds cork-\u003ebase.fragsize to IP6_MAX_MTU for UDP sockets to\navoid a possible overflow of UDP length that triggers a WARN in\nudp_set_len_short when setsockopt IPV6_MTU_DISCOVER is set to\nIPV6_PMTUDISC_DO or IPV6_PMTUDISC_PROBE, and a large packet is sent over\na netdev with an unusually large MTU.\n\nSteps to reproduce (included in the new selftest):\n\n1. Set device MTU bigger than IP6_MAX_MTU. cork-\u003ebase.fragsize will be\n   set to that MTU in ip6_setup_cork.\n2. Set IPV6_MTU_DISCOVER to IPV6_PMTUDISC_PROBE or IPV6_PMTUDISC_DO. It\n   lets maxnonfragsize be set to device MTU (cork-\u003efragsize) in\n   __ip6_append_data, rather than to IP6_MAX_MTU.\n3. Send 65528 bytes of payload (+8 bytes of UDP header, +40 bytes of\n   IPv6 header). Device MTU allows it (it's only one byte bigger than\n   IP6_MAX_MTU, and the device MTU is bigger than that).\n4. The UDP length in the built packet is 65536, which overflows the\n   16-bit length field and triggers the WARN in udp_set_len_short.\n\nTo avoid breaking sending UDP jumbograms over raw IPv6 sockets, limit\nthe change to UDP sockets only.\n\nThe original overflow bug with IPv6 and IPV6_PMTUDISC_DO seems to\npredate git history (verified reproduction on 2.6.21), was fixed later,\nand then reappeared in commit 427faee167bc (\"net: ipv6: introduce\nip6_dst_mtu_maybe_forward\"), which is chosen as the Fixes tag here. The\noverflow with IPV6_PMTUDISC_PROBE reproduces since its introduction in\ncommit 628a5c561890 (\"[INET]: Add IP(V6)_PMTUDISC_RPOBE\")."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97984",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-97984\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-97984](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97984)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet: ipv6: Fix UDP length overflow with PMTU discover and big MTU\n\nThis commit bounds cork-\u003ebase.fragsize to IP6_MAX_MTU for UDP sockets to\navoid a possible overflow of UDP length that triggers a WARN in\nudp_set_len_short when setsockopt IPV6_MTU_DISCOVER is set to\nIPV6_PMTUDISC_DO or IPV6_PMTUDISC_PROBE, and a large packet is sent over\na netdev with an unusually large MTU.\n\nSteps to reproduce (included in the new selftest):\n\n1. Set device MTU bigger than IP6_MAX_MTU. cork-\u003ebase.fragsize will be\n   set to that MTU in ip6_setup_cork.\n2. Set IPV6_MTU_DISCOVER to IPV6_PMTUDISC_PROBE or IPV6_PMTUDISC_DO. It\n   lets maxnonfragsize be set to device MTU (cork-\u003efragsize) in\n   __ip6_append_data, rather than to IP6_MAX_MTU.\n3. Send 65528 bytes of payload (+8 bytes of UDP header, +40 bytes of\n   IPv6 header). Device MTU allows it (it's only one byte bigger than\n   IP6_MAX_MTU, and the device MTU is bigger than that).\n4. The UDP length in the built packet is 65536, which overflows the\n   16-bit length field and triggers the WARN in udp_set_len_short.\n\nTo avoid breaking sending UDP jumbograms over raw IPv6 sockets, limit\nthe change to UDP sockets only.\n\nThe original overflow bug with IPv6 and IPV6_PMTUDISC_DO seems to\npredate git history (verified reproduction on 2.6.21), was fixed later,\nand then reappeared in commit 427faee167bc (\"net: ipv6: introduce\nip6_dst_mtu_maybe_forward\"), which is chosen as the Fixes tag here. The\noverflow with IPV6_PMTUDISC_PROBE reproduces since its introduction in\ncommit 628a5c561890 (\"[INET]: Add IP(V6)_PMTUDISC_RPOBE\").",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-97984**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-97984](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97984)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet: ipv6: Fix UDP length overflow with PMTU discover and big MTU\n\nThis commit bounds cork-\u003ebase.fragsize to IP6_MAX_MTU for UDP sockets to\navoid a possible overflow of UDP length that triggers a WARN in\nudp_set_len_short when setsockopt IPV6_MTU_DISCOVER is set to\nIPV6_PMTUDISC_DO or IPV6_PMTUDISC_PROBE, and a large packet is sent over\na netdev with an unusually large MTU.\n\nSteps to reproduce (included in the new selftest):\n\n1. Set device MTU bigger than IP6_MAX_MTU. cork-\u003ebase.fragsize will be\n   set to that MTU in ip6_setup_cork.\n2. Set IPV6_MTU_DISCOVER to IPV6_PMTUDISC_PROBE or IPV6_PMTUDISC_DO. It\n   lets maxnonfragsize be set to device MTU (cork-\u003efragsize) in\n   __ip6_append_data, rather than to IP6_MAX_MTU.\n3. Send 65528 bytes of payload (+8 bytes of UDP header, +40 bytes of\n   IPv6 header). Device MTU allows it (it's only one byte bigger than\n   IP6_MAX_MTU, and the device MTU is bigger than that).\n4. The UDP length in the built packet is 65536, which overflows the\n   16-bit length field and triggers the WARN in udp_set_len_short.\n\nTo avoid breaking sending UDP jumbograms over raw IPv6 sockets, limit\nthe change to UDP sockets only.\n\nThe original overflow bug with IPv6 and IPV6_PMTUDISC_DO seems to\npredate git history (verified reproduction on 2.6.21), was fixed later,\nand then reappeared in commit 427faee167bc (\"net: ipv6: introduce\nip6_dst_mtu_maybe_forward\"), which is chosen as the Fixes tag here. The\noverflow with IPV6_PMTUDISC_PROBE reproduces since its introduction in\ncommit 628a5c561890 (\"[INET]: Add IP(V6)_PMTUDISC_RPOBE\")."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
