            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-98094",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:  s ..."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nstaging: fbtft: make dirty_lock IRQ-safe\n\nfbtft_mkdirty() can be reached from the fbcon rendering path while\nprocessing printk() in hardirq context. Meanwhile, dirty_lock is also\ntaken by fbtft_deferred_io() in workqueue context with local interrupts\nenabled.\n\nLockdep reports a possible IRQ lock inversion involving dirty_lock and\nconsole_owner. A hardirq can interrupt a CPU holding dirty_lock and\nenter the console rendering path, which can attempt to acquire\ndirty_lock again.\n\nThe following lockdep report was observed on an RK3566 system with\nCONFIG_PROVE_LOCKING enabled:\n\n  WARNING: possible irq lock inversion dependency detected\n  swapper/2/0 just changed the state of lock:\n  (console_owner){-...}-{0:0}\n  but this lock took another, HARDIRQ-unsafe lock in the past:\n  (\u0026par-\u003edirty_lock){+.+.}-{2:2}\n\n  CPU0                    CPU1\n  ----                    ----\n  lock(\u0026par-\u003edirty_lock);\n                         local_irq_disable();\n                         lock(console_owner);\n                         lock(\u0026par-\u003edirty_lock);\n  \u003cInterrupt\u003e\n    lock(console_owner);\n\n  *** DEADLOCK ***\n\nUse spin_lock_irqsave() for fbtft_mkdirty() and spin_lock_irq() for\nfbtft_deferred_io(). They only access the dirty line range, so the\nIRQ-off regions remain short."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98094",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-98094\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-98094](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98094)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nstaging: fbtft: make dirty_lock IRQ-safe\n\nfbtft_mkdirty() can be reached from the fbcon rendering path while\nprocessing printk() in hardirq context. Meanwhile, dirty_lock is also\ntaken by fbtft_deferred_io() in workqueue context with local interrupts\nenabled.\n\nLockdep reports a possible IRQ lock inversion involving dirty_lock and\nconsole_owner. A hardirq can interrupt a CPU holding dirty_lock and\nenter the console rendering path, which can attempt to acquire\ndirty_lock again.\n\nThe following lockdep report was observed on an RK3566 system with\nCONFIG_PROVE_LOCKING enabled:\n\n  WARNING: possible irq lock inversion dependency detected\n  swapper/2/0 just changed the state of lock:\n  (console_owner){-...}-{0:0}\n  but this lock took another, HARDIRQ-unsafe lock in the past:\n  (\u0026par-\u003edirty_lock){+.+.}-{2:2}\n\n  CPU0                    CPU1\n  ----                    ----\n  lock(\u0026par-\u003edirty_lock);\n                         local_irq_disable();\n                         lock(console_owner);\n                         lock(\u0026par-\u003edirty_lock);\n  \u003cInterrupt\u003e\n    lock(console_owner);\n\n  *** DEADLOCK ***\n\nUse spin_lock_irqsave() for fbtft_mkdirty() and spin_lock_irq() for\nfbtft_deferred_io(). They only access the dirty line range, so the\nIRQ-off regions remain short.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-98094**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-98094](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98094)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nstaging: fbtft: make dirty_lock IRQ-safe\n\nfbtft_mkdirty() can be reached from the fbcon rendering path while\nprocessing printk() in hardirq context. Meanwhile, dirty_lock is also\ntaken by fbtft_deferred_io() in workqueue context with local interrupts\nenabled.\n\nLockdep reports a possible IRQ lock inversion involving dirty_lock and\nconsole_owner. A hardirq can interrupt a CPU holding dirty_lock and\nenter the console rendering path, which can attempt to acquire\ndirty_lock again.\n\nThe following lockdep report was observed on an RK3566 system with\nCONFIG_PROVE_LOCKING enabled:\n\n  WARNING: possible irq lock inversion dependency detected\n  swapper/2/0 just changed the state of lock:\n  (console_owner){-...}-{0:0}\n  but this lock took another, HARDIRQ-unsafe lock in the past:\n  (\u0026par-\u003edirty_lock){+.+.}-{2:2}\n\n  CPU0                    CPU1\n  ----                    ----\n  lock(\u0026par-\u003edirty_lock);\n                         local_irq_disable();\n                         lock(console_owner);\n                         lock(\u0026par-\u003edirty_lock);\n  \u003cInterrupt\u003e\n    lock(console_owner);\n\n  *** DEADLOCK ***\n\nUse spin_lock_irqsave() for fbtft_mkdirty() and spin_lock_irq() for\nfbtft_deferred_io(). They only access the dirty line range, so the\nIRQ-off regions remain short."
              },
