            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-98428",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: ALSA: usb: 6fire: Avoid embedded URBs"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nALSA: usb: 6fire: Avoid embedded URBs\n\nThe USB 6fire driver uses URBs embedded in different structs for PCM,\nMIDI and communication, and this is basically a buggy implementation\nnowadays; since a URB is managed with a refcount, this may lead to a\nUAF when the URB is released asynchronously.\n\nFor addressing the problem, this patch converts those embedded URBs to\nones that are properly allocated via usb_alloc_urb().  The\npcm_urb.packets[] is gone, as it's allocated by usb_alloc_urb(), hence\nit's found in urb.iso_frame_desc[] instead.\n\nThe conversions are rather straightforward; each embedded struct urb\nis changed to a pointer, and its callers are updated accordingly.\nThe resource for those structs are released in the common destructor\nfunctions (usb6fire_comm_free(), etc), which are called at both the\ninit error path and the disconnect.\n\nNo functional changes, only compile-tested."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98428",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-98428\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-98428](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98428)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nALSA: usb: 6fire: Avoid embedded URBs\n\nThe USB 6fire driver uses URBs embedded in different structs for PCM,\nMIDI and communication, and this is basically a buggy implementation\nnowadays; since a URB is managed with a refcount, this may lead to a\nUAF when the URB is released asynchronously.\n\nFor addressing the problem, this patch converts those embedded URBs to\nones that are properly allocated via usb_alloc_urb().  The\npcm_urb.packets[] is gone, as it's allocated by usb_alloc_urb(), hence\nit's found in urb.iso_frame_desc[] instead.\n\nThe conversions are rather straightforward; each embedded struct urb\nis changed to a pointer, and its callers are updated accordingly.\nThe resource for those structs are released in the common destructor\nfunctions (usb6fire_comm_free(), etc), which are called at both the\ninit error path and the disconnect.\n\nNo functional changes, only compile-tested.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-98428**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-98428](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98428)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nALSA: usb: 6fire: Avoid embedded URBs\n\nThe USB 6fire driver uses URBs embedded in different structs for PCM,\nMIDI and communication, and this is basically a buggy implementation\nnowadays; since a URB is managed with a refcount, this may lead to a\nUAF when the URB is released asynchronously.\n\nFor addressing the problem, this patch converts those embedded URBs to\nones that are properly allocated via usb_alloc_urb().  The\npcm_urb.packets[] is gone, as it's allocated by usb_alloc_urb(), hence\nit's found in urb.iso_frame_desc[] instead.\n\nThe conversions are rather straightforward; each embedded struct urb\nis changed to a pointer, and its callers are updated accordingly.\nThe resource for those structs are released in the common destructor\nfunctions (usb6fire_comm_free(), etc), which are called at both the\ninit error path and the disconnect.\n\nNo functional changes, only compile-tested."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-98429",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: RDMA/efa: Keep EQ resources alive while IRQ is registered"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nRDMA/efa: Keep EQ resources alive while IRQ is registered\n\nThe completion IRQ handler accesses the EQ state and DMA buffer. Its IRQ was\nregistered before that state was initialized, while teardown released the\nbuffer before free_irq() synchronized the handler.\n\nInitialize the EQ without arming it, register the IRQ, and then arm it.\nReverse the resource order during teardown by freeing the IRQ before\ndestroying the EQ."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98429",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-98429\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-98429](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98429)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nRDMA/efa: Keep EQ resources alive while IRQ is registered\n\nThe completion IRQ handler accesses the EQ state and DMA buffer. Its IRQ was\nregistered before that state was initialized, while teardown released the\nbuffer before free_irq() synchronized the handler.\n\nInitialize the EQ without arming it, register the IRQ, and then arm it.\nReverse the resource order during teardown by freeing the IRQ before\ndestroying the EQ.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-98429**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-98429](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98429)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nRDMA/efa: Keep EQ resources alive while IRQ is registered\n\nThe completion IRQ handler accesses the EQ state and DMA buffer. Its IRQ was\nregistered before that state was initialized, while teardown released the\nbuffer before free_irq() synchronized the handler.\n\nInitialize the EQ without arming it, register the IRQ, and then arm it.\nReverse the resource order during teardown by freeing the IRQ before\ndestroying the EQ."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-99999",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: ipv4: validate checksum_start before completing checksum"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nipv4: validate checksum_start before completing checksum\n\nIf a packet with bad checksum metadata gets into the ipv4 stack,\nskb_checksum_help can corrupt the network header and cause a bunch of\nmischief.\n\nThis was discovered and reported by Paulos, and has been reporoduced\nby others independently since.\n\nWe really shouldn't allow such packets in, but as a defence\nin depth measure, let's also check before we complete the checksum.\n\nA more complete validation at input is forthcoming, but needs more work."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-99999",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-99999\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-99999](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-99999)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nipv4: validate checksum_start before completing checksum\n\nIf a packet with bad checksum metadata gets into the ipv4 stack,\nskb_checksum_help can corrupt the network header and cause a bunch of\nmischief.\n\nThis was discovered and reported by Paulos, and has been reporoduced\nby others independently since.\n\nWe really shouldn't allow such packets in, but as a defence\nin depth measure, let's also check before we complete the checksum.\n\nA more complete validation at input is forthcoming, but needs more work.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-99999**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-99999](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-99999)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nipv4: validate checksum_start before completing checksum\n\nIf a packet with bad checksum metadata gets into the ipv4 stack,\nskb_checksum_help can corrupt the network header and cause a bunch of\nmischief.\n\nThis was discovered and reported by Paulos, and has been reporoduced\nby others independently since.\n\nWe really shouldn't allow such packets in, but as a defence\nin depth measure, let's also check before we complete the checksum.\n\nA more complete validation at input is forthcoming, but needs more work."
              },
