            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-68338",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: net: dsa: microchip: Don't free uninitialized ksz_irq"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet: dsa: microchip: Don't free uninitialized ksz_irq\n\nIf something goes wrong at setup, ksz_irq_free() can be called on\nuninitialized ksz_irq (for example when ksz_ptp_irq_setup() fails). It\nleads to freeing uninitialized IRQ numbers and/or domains.\n\nUse dsa_switch_for_each_user_port_continue_reverse() in the error path\nto iterate only over the fully initialized ports."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68338",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-68338\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2025-68338](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68338)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet: dsa: microchip: Don't free uninitialized ksz_irq\n\nIf something goes wrong at setup, ksz_irq_free() can be called on\nuninitialized ksz_irq (for example when ksz_ptp_irq_setup() fails). It\nleads to freeing uninitialized IRQ numbers and/or domains.\n\nUse dsa_switch_for_each_user_port_continue_reverse() in the error path\nto iterate only over the fully initialized ports.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-68338**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2025-68338](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68338)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet: dsa: microchip: Don't free uninitialized ksz_irq\n\nIf something goes wrong at setup, ksz_irq_free() can be called on\nuninitialized ksz_irq (for example when ksz_ptp_irq_setup() fails). It\nleads to freeing uninitialized IRQ numbers and/or domains.\n\nUse dsa_switch_for_each_user_port_continue_reverse() in the error path\nto iterate only over the fully initialized ports."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-68376",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: coresight: ETR: Fix ETR buffer use-after-free issue"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ncoresight: ETR: Fix ETR buffer use-after-free issue\n\nWhen ETR is enabled as CS_MODE_SYSFS, if the buffer size is changed\nand enabled again, currently sysfs_buf will point to the newly\nallocated memory(buf_new) and free the old memory(buf_old). But the\netr_buf that is being used by the ETR remains pointed to buf_old, not\nupdated to buf_new. In this case, it will result in a memory\nuse-after-free issue.\n\nFix this by checking ETR's mode before updating and releasing buf_old,\nif the mode is CS_MODE_SYSFS, then skip updating and releasing it."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68376",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-68376\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2025-68376](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68376)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ncoresight: ETR: Fix ETR buffer use-after-free issue\n\nWhen ETR is enabled as CS_MODE_SYSFS, if the buffer size is changed\nand enabled again, currently sysfs_buf will point to the newly\nallocated memory(buf_new) and free the old memory(buf_old). But the\netr_buf that is being used by the ETR remains pointed to buf_old, not\nupdated to buf_new. In this case, it will result in a memory\nuse-after-free issue.\n\nFix this by checking ETR's mode before updating and releasing buf_old,\nif the mode is CS_MODE_SYSFS, then skip updating and releasing it.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-68376**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2025-68376](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68376)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ncoresight: ETR: Fix ETR buffer use-after-free issue\n\nWhen ETR is enabled as CS_MODE_SYSFS, if the buffer size is changed\nand enabled again, currently sysfs_buf will point to the newly\nallocated memory(buf_new) and free the old memory(buf_old). But the\netr_buf that is being used by the ETR remains pointed to buf_old, not\nupdated to buf_new. In this case, it will result in a memory\nuse-after-free issue.\n\nFix this by checking ETR's mode before updating and releasing buf_old,\nif the mode is CS_MODE_SYSFS, then skip updating and releasing it."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-68781",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: usb: phy: fsl-usb: Fix use-after-free in delayed work during device removal"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nusb: phy: fsl-usb: Fix use-after-free in delayed work during device removal\n\nThe delayed work item otg_event is initialized in fsl_otg_conf() and\nscheduled under two conditions:\n1. When a host controller binds to the OTG controller.\n2. When the USB ID pin state changes (cable insertion/removal).\n\nA race condition occurs when the device is removed via fsl_otg_remove():\nthe fsl_otg instance may be freed while the delayed work is still pending\nor executing. This leads to use-after-free when the work function\nfsl_otg_event() accesses the already freed memory.\n\nThe problematic scenario:\n\n(detach thread)            | (delayed work)\nfsl_otg_remove()           |\n  kfree(fsl_otg_dev) //FREE| fsl_otg_event()\n                           |   og = container_of(...) //USE\n                           |   og-\u003e //USE\n\nFix this by calling disable_delayed_work_sync() in fsl_otg_remove()\nbefore deallocating the fsl_otg structure. This ensures the delayed work\nis properly canceled and completes execution prior to memory deallocation.\n\nThis bug was identified through static analysis."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68781",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-68781\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2025-68781](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68781)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nusb: phy: fsl-usb: Fix use-after-free in delayed work during device removal\n\nThe delayed work item otg_event is initialized in fsl_otg_conf() and\nscheduled under two conditions:\n1. When a host controller binds to the OTG controller.\n2. When the USB ID pin state changes (cable insertion/removal).\n\nA race condition occurs when the device is removed via fsl_otg_remove():\nthe fsl_otg instance may be freed while the delayed work is still pending\nor executing. This leads to use-after-free when the work function\nfsl_otg_event() accesses the already freed memory.\n\nThe problematic scenario:\n\n(detach thread)            | (delayed work)\nfsl_otg_remove()           |\n  kfree(fsl_otg_dev) //FREE| fsl_otg_event()\n                           |   og = container_of(...) //USE\n                           |   og-\u003e //USE\n\nFix this by calling disable_delayed_work_sync() in fsl_otg_remove()\nbefore deallocating the fsl_otg structure. This ensures the delayed work\nis properly canceled and completes execution prior to memory deallocation.\n\nThis bug was identified through static analysis.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-68781**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2025-68781](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-68781)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nusb: phy: fsl-usb: Fix use-after-free in delayed work during device removal\n\nThe delayed work item otg_event is initialized in fsl_otg_conf() and\nscheduled under two conditions:\n1. When a host controller binds to the OTG controller.\n2. When the USB ID pin state changes (cable insertion/removal).\n\nA race condition occurs when the device is removed via fsl_otg_remove():\nthe fsl_otg instance may be freed while the delayed work is still pending\nor executing. This leads to use-after-free when the work function\nfsl_otg_event() accesses the already freed memory.\n\nThe problematic scenario:\n\n(detach thread)            | (delayed work)\nfsl_otg_remove()           |\n  kfree(fsl_otg_dev) //FREE| fsl_otg_event()\n                           |   og = container_of(...) //USE\n                           |   og-\u003e //USE\n\nFix this by calling disable_delayed_work_sync() in fsl_otg_remove()\nbefore deallocating the fsl_otg structure. This ensures the delayed work\nis properly canceled and completes execution prior to memory deallocation.\n\nThis bug was identified through static analysis."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-71313",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: PCI: endpoint: Add missing NULL check for alloc_workqueue()"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nPCI: endpoint: Add missing NULL check for alloc_workqueue()\n\nalloc_workqueue() can return NULL on memory allocation failure. Without\nproper error checking, this may lead to a NULL pointer dereference when\nqueue_work() is later called with the NULL workqueue pointer in\nepf_ntb_epc_init().\n\nAdd a NULL check immediately after alloc_workqueue() and return -ENOMEM on\nfailure to prevent the driver from loading with an invalid workqueue\npointer."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-71313",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-71313\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2025-71313](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-71313)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nPCI: endpoint: Add missing NULL check for alloc_workqueue()\n\nalloc_workqueue() can return NULL on memory allocation failure. Without\nproper error checking, this may lead to a NULL pointer dereference when\nqueue_work() is later called with the NULL workqueue pointer in\nepf_ntb_epc_init().\n\nAdd a NULL check immediately after alloc_workqueue() and return -ENOMEM on\nfailure to prevent the driver from loading with an invalid workqueue\npointer.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-71313**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2025-71313](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-71313)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nPCI: endpoint: Add missing NULL check for alloc_workqueue()\n\nalloc_workqueue() can return NULL on memory allocation failure. Without\nproper error checking, this may lead to a NULL pointer dereference when\nqueue_work() is later called with the NULL workqueue pointer in\nepf_ntb_epc_init().\n\nAdd a NULL check immediately after alloc_workqueue() and return -ENOMEM on\nfailure to prevent the driver from loading with an invalid workqueue\npointer."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-100072",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: ACPI: platform: Use acpi_bus_get_primary_device()"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nACPI: platform: Use acpi_bus_get_primary_device()\n\nThe acpi_get_first_physical_node() usage in acpi_platform_fill_resource()\nand acpi_create_platform_device() is generally unsafe because in theory\nthe device returned by it may be freed at any time [1].\n\nIt is also inefficient because acpi_get_first_physical_node() is called\nmultiple times for the same argument which can be avoided.\n\nAddress these issues by using acpi_bus_get_primary_device() instead of\nacpi_get_first_physical_node() and adjusting the code to call it just\nonce at the beginning of and acpi_create_platform_device() and drop\nthe device reference acquired by it upon the return from that function."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-100072",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-100072\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-100072](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-100072)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nACPI: platform: Use acpi_bus_get_primary_device()\n\nThe acpi_get_first_physical_node() usage in acpi_platform_fill_resource()\nand acpi_create_platform_device() is generally unsafe because in theory\nthe device returned by it may be freed at any time [1].\n\nIt is also inefficient because acpi_get_first_physical_node() is called\nmultiple times for the same argument which can be avoided.\n\nAddress these issues by using acpi_bus_get_primary_device() instead of\nacpi_get_first_physical_node() and adjusting the code to call it just\nonce at the beginning of and acpi_create_platform_device() and drop\nthe device reference acquired by it upon the return from that function.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-100072**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-100072](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-100072)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nACPI: platform: Use acpi_bus_get_primary_device()\n\nThe acpi_get_first_physical_node() usage in acpi_platform_fill_resource()\nand acpi_create_platform_device() is generally unsafe because in theory\nthe device returned by it may be freed at any time [1].\n\nIt is also inefficient because acpi_get_first_physical_node() is called\nmultiple times for the same argument which can be avoided.\n\nAddress these issues by using acpi_bus_get_primary_device() instead of\nacpi_get_first_physical_node() and adjusting the code to call it just\nonce at the beginning of and acpi_create_platform_device() and drop\nthe device reference acquired by it upon the return from that function."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-23017",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: idpf: fix error handling in the init_task on load"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nidpf: fix error handling in the init_task on load\n\nIf the init_task fails during a driver load, we end up without vports and\nnetdevs, effectively failing the entire process. In that state a\nsubsequent reset will result in a crash as the service task attempts to\naccess uninitialized resources. Following trace is from an error in the\ninit_task where the CREATE_VPORT (op 501) is rejected by the FW:\n\n[40922.763136] idpf 0000:83:00.0: Device HW Reset initiated\n[40924.449797] idpf 0000:83:00.0: Transaction failed (op 501)\n[40958.148190] idpf 0000:83:00.0: HW reset detected\n[40958.161202] BUG: kernel NULL pointer dereference, address: 00000000000000a8\n...\n[40958.168094] Workqueue: idpf-0000:83:00.0-vc_event idpf_vc_event_task [idpf]\n[40958.168865] RIP: 0010:idpf_vc_event_task+0x9b/0x350 [idpf]\n...\n[40958.177932] Call Trace:\n[40958.178491]  \u003cTASK\u003e\n[40958.179040]  process_one_work+0x226/0x6d0\n[40958.179609]  worker_thread+0x19e/0x340\n[40958.180158]  ? __pfx_worker_thread+0x10/0x10\n[40958.180702]  kthread+0x10f/0x250\n[40958.181238]  ? __pfx_kthread+0x10/0x10\n[40958.181774]  ret_from_fork+0x251/0x2b0\n[40958.182307]  ? __pfx_kthread+0x10/0x10\n[40958.182834]  ret_from_fork_asm+0x1a/0x30\n[40958.183370]  \u003c/TASK\u003e\n\nFix the error handling in the init_task to make sure the service and\nmailbox tasks are disabled if the error happens during load. These are\nstarted in idpf_vc_core_init(), which spawns the init_task and has no way\nof knowing if it failed. If the error happens on reset, following\nsuccessful driver load, the tasks can still run, as that will allow the\nnetdevs to attempt recovery through another reset. Stop the PTP callbacks\neither way as those will be restarted by the call to idpf_vc_core_init()\nduring a successful reset."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-23017",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-23017\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-23017](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-23017)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nidpf: fix error handling in the init_task on load\n\nIf the init_task fails during a driver load, we end up without vports and\nnetdevs, effectively failing the entire process. In that state a\nsubsequent reset will result in a crash as the service task attempts to\naccess uninitialized resources. Following trace is from an error in the\ninit_task where the CREATE_VPORT (op 501) is rejected by the FW:\n\n[40922.763136] idpf 0000:83:00.0: Device HW Reset initiated\n[40924.449797] idpf 0000:83:00.0: Transaction failed (op 501)\n[40958.148190] idpf 0000:83:00.0: HW reset detected\n[40958.161202] BUG: kernel NULL pointer dereference, address: 00000000000000a8\n...\n[40958.168094] Workqueue: idpf-0000:83:00.0-vc_event idpf_vc_event_task [idpf]\n[40958.168865] RIP: 0010:idpf_vc_event_task+0x9b/0x350 [idpf]\n...\n[40958.177932] Call Trace:\n[40958.178491]  \u003cTASK\u003e\n[40958.179040]  process_one_work+0x226/0x6d0\n[40958.179609]  worker_thread+0x19e/0x340\n[40958.180158]  ? __pfx_worker_thread+0x10/0x10\n[40958.180702]  kthread+0x10f/0x250\n[40958.181238]  ? __pfx_kthread+0x10/0x10\n[40958.181774]  ret_from_fork+0x251/0x2b0\n[40958.182307]  ? __pfx_kthread+0x10/0x10\n[40958.182834]  ret_from_fork_asm+0x1a/0x30\n[40958.183370]  \u003c/TASK\u003e\n\nFix the error handling in the init_task to make sure the service and\nmailbox tasks are disabled if the error happens during load. These are\nstarted in idpf_vc_core_init(), which spawns the init_task and has no way\nof knowing if it failed. If the error happens on reset, following\nsuccessful driver load, the tasks can still run, as that will allow the\nnetdevs to attempt recovery through another reset. Stop the PTP callbacks\neither way as those will be restarted by the call to idpf_vc_core_init()\nduring a successful reset.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-23017**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-23017](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-23017)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nidpf: fix error handling in the init_task on load\n\nIf the init_task fails during a driver load, we end up without vports and\nnetdevs, effectively failing the entire process. In that state a\nsubsequent reset will result in a crash as the service task attempts to\naccess uninitialized resources. Following trace is from an error in the\ninit_task where the CREATE_VPORT (op 501) is rejected by the FW:\n\n[40922.763136] idpf 0000:83:00.0: Device HW Reset initiated\n[40924.449797] idpf 0000:83:00.0: Transaction failed (op 501)\n[40958.148190] idpf 0000:83:00.0: HW reset detected\n[40958.161202] BUG: kernel NULL pointer dereference, address: 00000000000000a8\n...\n[40958.168094] Workqueue: idpf-0000:83:00.0-vc_event idpf_vc_event_task [idpf]\n[40958.168865] RIP: 0010:idpf_vc_event_task+0x9b/0x350 [idpf]\n...\n[40958.177932] Call Trace:\n[40958.178491]  \u003cTASK\u003e\n[40958.179040]  process_one_work+0x226/0x6d0\n[40958.179609]  worker_thread+0x19e/0x340\n[40958.180158]  ? __pfx_worker_thread+0x10/0x10\n[40958.180702]  kthread+0x10f/0x250\n[40958.181238]  ? __pfx_kthread+0x10/0x10\n[40958.181774]  ret_from_fork+0x251/0x2b0\n[40958.182307]  ? __pfx_kthread+0x10/0x10\n[40958.182834]  ret_from_fork_asm+0x1a/0x30\n[40958.183370]  \u003c/TASK\u003e\n\nFix the error handling in the init_task to make sure the service and\nmailbox tasks are disabled if the error happens during load. These are\nstarted in idpf_vc_core_init(), which spawns the init_task and has no way\nof knowing if it failed. If the error happens on reset, following\nsuccessful driver load, the tasks can still run, as that will allow the\nnetdevs to attempt recovery through another reset. Stop the PTP callbacks\neither way as those will be restarted by the call to idpf_vc_core_init()\nduring a successful reset."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-23137",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: of: unittest: Fix memory leak in unittest_data_add()"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nof: unittest: Fix memory leak in unittest_data_add()\n\nIn unittest_data_add(), if of_resolve_phandles() fails, the allocated\nunittest_data is not freed, leading to a memory leak.\n\nFix this by using scope-based cleanup helper __free(kfree) for automatic\nresource cleanup. This ensures unittest_data is automatically freed when\nit goes out of scope in error paths.\n\nFor the success path, use retain_and_null_ptr() to transfer ownership\nof the memory to the device tree and prevent double freeing."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-23137",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-23137\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-23137](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-23137)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nof: unittest: Fix memory leak in unittest_data_add()\n\nIn unittest_data_add(), if of_resolve_phandles() fails, the allocated\nunittest_data is not freed, leading to a memory leak.\n\nFix this by using scope-based cleanup helper __free(kfree) for automatic\nresource cleanup. This ensures unittest_data is automatically freed when\nit goes out of scope in error paths.\n\nFor the success path, use retain_and_null_ptr() to transfer ownership\nof the memory to the device tree and prevent double freeing.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-23137**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-23137](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-23137)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nof: unittest: Fix memory leak in unittest_data_add()\n\nIn unittest_data_add(), if of_resolve_phandles() fails, the allocated\nunittest_data is not freed, leading to a memory leak.\n\nFix this by using scope-based cleanup helper __free(kfree) for automatic\nresource cleanup. This ensures unittest_data is automatically freed when\nit goes out of scope in error paths.\n\nFor the success path, use retain_and_null_ptr() to transfer ownership\nof the memory to the device tree and prevent double freeing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-23152",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: wifi: mac80211: correctly decode TTLM with default link map"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: mac80211: correctly decode TTLM with default link map\n\nTID-To-Link Mapping (TTLM) elements do not contain any link mapping\npresence indicator if a default mapping is used and parsing needs to be\nskipped.\n\nNote that access points should not explicitly report an advertised TTLM\nwith a default mapping as that is the implied mapping if the element is\nnot included, this is even the case when switching back to the default\nmapping. However, mac80211 would incorrectly parse the frame and would\nalso read one byte beyond the end of the element."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-23152",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-23152\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-23152](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-23152)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: mac80211: correctly decode TTLM with default link map\n\nTID-To-Link Mapping (TTLM) elements do not contain any link mapping\npresence indicator if a default mapping is used and parsing needs to be\nskipped.\n\nNote that access points should not explicitly report an advertised TTLM\nwith a default mapping as that is the implied mapping if the element is\nnot included, this is even the case when switching back to the default\nmapping. However, mac80211 would incorrectly parse the frame and would\nalso read one byte beyond the end of the element.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-23152**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-23152](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-23152)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: mac80211: correctly decode TTLM with default link map\n\nTID-To-Link Mapping (TTLM) elements do not contain any link mapping\npresence indicator if a default mapping is used and parsing needs to be\nskipped.\n\nNote that access points should not explicitly report an advertised TTLM\nwith a default mapping as that is the implied mapping if the element is\nnot included, this is even the case when switching back to the default\nmapping. However, mac80211 would incorrectly parse the frame and would\nalso read one byte beyond the end of the element."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-23217",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: riscv: trace: fix snapshot deadlock with sbi ecall"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nriscv: trace: fix snapshot deadlock with sbi ecall\n\nIf sbi_ecall.c's functions are traceable,\n\necho \"__sbi_ecall:snapshot\" \u003e /sys/kernel/tracing/set_ftrace_filter\n\nmay get the kernel into a deadlock.\n\n(Functions in sbi_ecall.c are excluded from tracing if\nCONFIG_RISCV_ALTERNATIVE_EARLY is set.)\n\n__sbi_ecall triggers a snapshot of the ringbuffer. The snapshot code\nraises an IPI interrupt, which results in another call to __sbi_ecall\nand another snapshot...\n\nAll it takes to get into this endless loop is one initial __sbi_ecall.\nOn RISC-V systems without SSTC extension, the clock events in\ntimer-riscv.c issue periodic sbi ecalls, making the problem easy to\ntrigger.\n\nAlways exclude the sbi_ecall.c functions from tracing to fix the\npotential deadlock.\n\nsbi ecalls can easiliy be logged via trace events, excluding ecall\nfunctions from function tracing is not a big limitation."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-23217",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-23217\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-23217](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-23217)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nriscv: trace: fix snapshot deadlock with sbi ecall\n\nIf sbi_ecall.c's functions are traceable,\n\necho \"__sbi_ecall:snapshot\" \u003e /sys/kernel/tracing/set_ftrace_filter\n\nmay get the kernel into a deadlock.\n\n(Functions in sbi_ecall.c are excluded from tracing if\nCONFIG_RISCV_ALTERNATIVE_EARLY is set.)\n\n__sbi_ecall triggers a snapshot of the ringbuffer. The snapshot code\nraises an IPI interrupt, which results in another call to __sbi_ecall\nand another snapshot...\n\nAll it takes to get into this endless loop is one initial __sbi_ecall.\nOn RISC-V systems without SSTC extension, the clock events in\ntimer-riscv.c issue periodic sbi ecalls, making the problem easy to\ntrigger.\n\nAlways exclude the sbi_ecall.c functions from tracing to fix the\npotential deadlock.\n\nsbi ecalls can easiliy be logged via trace events, excluding ecall\nfunctions from function tracing is not a big limitation.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-23217**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-23217](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-23217)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nriscv: trace: fix snapshot deadlock with sbi ecall\n\nIf sbi_ecall.c's functions are traceable,\n\necho \"__sbi_ecall:snapshot\" \u003e /sys/kernel/tracing/set_ftrace_filter\n\nmay get the kernel into a deadlock.\n\n(Functions in sbi_ecall.c are excluded from tracing if\nCONFIG_RISCV_ALTERNATIVE_EARLY is set.)\n\n__sbi_ecall triggers a snapshot of the ringbuffer. The snapshot code\nraises an IPI interrupt, which results in another call to __sbi_ecall\nand another snapshot...\n\nAll it takes to get into this endless loop is one initial __sbi_ecall.\nOn RISC-V systems without SSTC extension, the clock events in\ntimer-riscv.c issue periodic sbi ecalls, making the problem easy to\ntrigger.\n\nAlways exclude the sbi_ecall.c functions from tracing to fix the\npotential deadlock.\n\nsbi ecalls can easiliy be logged via trace events, excluding ecall\nfunctions from function tracing is not a big limitation."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-23327",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: cxl/mbox: validate payload size before accessing contents in cxl_payload_from_user_allowed()"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ncxl/mbox: validate payload size before accessing contents in cxl_payload_from_user_allowed()\n\ncxl_payload_from_user_allowed() casts and dereferences the input\npayload without first verifying its size. When a raw mailbox command\nis sent with an undersized payload (ie: 1 byte for CXL_MBOX_OP_CLEAR_LOG,\nwhich expects a 16-byte UUID), uuid_equal() reads past the allocated buffer,\ntriggering a KASAN splat:\n\nBUG: KASAN: slab-out-of-bounds in memcmp+0x176/0x1d0 lib/string.c:683\nRead of size 8 at addr ffff88810130f5c0 by task syz.1.62/2258\n\nCPU: 2 UID: 0 PID: 2258 Comm: syz.1.62 Not tainted 6.19.0-dirty #3 PREEMPT(voluntary)\nHardware name: QEMU Standard PC (Q35 + ICH9, 2009), BIOS rel-1.17.0-0-gb52ca86e094d-prebuilt.qemu.org 04/01/2014\nCall Trace:\n \u003cTASK\u003e\n __dump_stack lib/dump_stack.c:94 [inline]\n dump_stack_lvl+0xab/0xe0 lib/dump_stack.c:120\n print_address_description mm/kasan/report.c:378 [inline]\n print_report+0xce/0x650 mm/kasan/report.c:482\n kasan_report+0xce/0x100 mm/kasan/report.c:595\n memcmp+0x176/0x1d0 lib/string.c:683\n uuid_equal include/linux/uuid.h:73 [inline]\n cxl_payload_from_user_allowed drivers/cxl/core/mbox.c:345 [inline]\n cxl_mbox_cmd_ctor drivers/cxl/core/mbox.c:368 [inline]\n cxl_validate_cmd_from_user drivers/cxl/core/mbox.c:522 [inline]\n cxl_send_cmd+0x9c0/0xb50 drivers/cxl/core/mbox.c:643\n __cxl_memdev_ioctl drivers/cxl/core/memdev.c:698 [inline]\n cxl_memdev_ioctl+0x14f/0x190 drivers/cxl/core/memdev.c:713\n vfs_ioctl fs/ioctl.c:51 [inline]\n __do_sys_ioctl fs/ioctl.c:597 [inline]\n __se_sys_ioctl fs/ioctl.c:583 [inline]\n __x64_sys_ioctl+0x18e/0x210 fs/ioctl.c:583\n do_syscall_x64 arch/x86/entry/syscall_64.c:63 [inline]\n do_syscall_64+0xa8/0x330 arch/x86/entry/syscall_64.c:94\n entry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x77/0x7f\nRIP: 0033:0x7fdaf331ba79\nCode: ff ff c3 66 2e 0f 1f 84 00 00 00 00 00 0f 1f 40 00 48 89 f8 48 89 f7 48 89 d6 48 89 ca 4d 89 c2 4d 89 c8 4c 8b 4c 24 08 0f 05 \u003c48\u003e 3d 01 f0 ff ff 73 01 c3 48 c7 c1 a8 ff ff ff f7 d8 64 89 01 48\nRSP: 002b:00007fdaf1d77038 EFLAGS: 00000246 ORIG_RAX: 0000000000000010\nRAX: ffffffffffffffda RBX: 00007fdaf3585fa0 RCX: 00007fdaf331ba79\nRDX: 00002000000001c0 RSI: 00000000c030ce02 RDI: 0000000000000003\nRBP: 00007fdaf33749df R08: 0000000000000000 R09: 0000000000000000\nR10: 0000000000000000 R11: 0000000000000246 R12: 0000000000000000\nR13: 00007fdaf3586038 R14: 00007fdaf3585fa0 R15: 00007ffced2af768\n \u003c/TASK\u003e\n\nAdd 'in_size' parameter to cxl_payload_from_user_allowed() and validate\nthe payload is large enough."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-23327",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-23327\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-23327](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-23327)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ncxl/mbox: validate payload size before accessing contents in cxl_payload_from_user_allowed()\n\ncxl_payload_from_user_allowed() casts and dereferences the input\npayload without first verifying its size. When a raw mailbox command\nis sent with an undersized payload (ie: 1 byte for CXL_MBOX_OP_CLEAR_LOG,\nwhich expects a 16-byte UUID), uuid_equal() reads past the allocated buffer,\ntriggering a KASAN splat:\n\nBUG: KASAN: slab-out-of-bounds in memcmp+0x176/0x1d0 lib/string.c:683\nRead of size 8 at addr ffff88810130f5c0 by task syz.1.62/2258\n\nCPU: 2 UID: 0 PID: 2258 Comm: syz.1.62 Not tainted 6.19.0-dirty #3 PREEMPT(voluntary)\nHardware name: QEMU Standard PC (Q35 + ICH9, 2009), BIOS rel-1.17.0-0-gb52ca86e094d-prebuilt.qemu.org 04/01/2014\nCall Trace:\n \u003cTASK\u003e\n __dump_stack lib/dump_stack.c:94 [inline]\n dump_stack_lvl+0xab/0xe0 lib/dump_stack.c:120\n print_address_description mm/kasan/report.c:378 [inline]\n print_report+0xce/0x650 mm/kasan/report.c:482\n kasan_report+0xce/0x100 mm/kasan/report.c:595\n memcmp+0x176/0x1d0 lib/string.c:683\n uuid_equal include/linux/uuid.h:73 [inline]\n cxl_payload_from_user_allowed drivers/cxl/core/mbox.c:345 [inline]\n cxl_mbox_cmd_ctor drivers/cxl/core/mbox.c:368 [inline]\n cxl_validate_cmd_from_user drivers/cxl/core/mbox.c:522 [inline]\n cxl_send_cmd+0x9c0/0xb50 drivers/cxl/core/mbox.c:643\n __cxl_memdev_ioctl drivers/cxl/core/memdev.c:698 [inline]\n cxl_memdev_ioctl+0x14f/0x190 drivers/cxl/core/memdev.c:713\n vfs_ioctl fs/ioctl.c:51 [inline]\n __do_sys_ioctl fs/ioctl.c:597 [inline]\n __se_sys_ioctl fs/ioctl.c:583 [inline]\n __x64_sys_ioctl+0x18e/0x210 fs/ioctl.c:583\n do_syscall_x64 arch/x86/entry/syscall_64.c:63 [inline]\n do_syscall_64+0xa8/0x330 arch/x86/entry/syscall_64.c:94\n entry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x77/0x7f\nRIP: 0033:0x7fdaf331ba79\nCode: ff ff c3 66 2e 0f 1f 84 00 00 00 00 00 0f 1f 40 00 48 89 f8 48 89 f7 48 89 d6 48 89 ca 4d 89 c2 4d 89 c8 4c 8b 4c 24 08 0f 05 \u003c48\u003e 3d 01 f0 ff ff 73 01 c3 48 c7 c1 a8 ff ff ff f7 d8 64 89 01 48\nRSP: 002b:00007fdaf1d77038 EFLAGS: 00000246 ORIG_RAX: 0000000000000010\nRAX: ffffffffffffffda RBX: 00007fdaf3585fa0 RCX: 00007fdaf331ba79\nRDX: 00002000000001c0 RSI: 00000000c030ce02 RDI: 0000000000000003\nRBP: 00007fdaf33749df R08: 0000000000000000 R09: 0000000000000000\nR10: 0000000000000000 R11: 0000000000000246 R12: 0000000000000000\nR13: 00007fdaf3586038 R14: 00007fdaf3585fa0 R15: 00007ffced2af768\n \u003c/TASK\u003e\n\nAdd 'in_size' parameter to cxl_payload_from_user_allowed() and validate\nthe payload is large enough.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-23327**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-23327](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-23327)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ncxl/mbox: validate payload size before accessing contents in cxl_payload_from_user_allowed()\n\ncxl_payload_from_user_allowed() casts and dereferences the input\npayload without first verifying its size. When a raw mailbox command\nis sent with an undersized payload (ie: 1 byte for CXL_MBOX_OP_CLEAR_LOG,\nwhich expects a 16-byte UUID), uuid_equal() reads past the allocated buffer,\ntriggering a KASAN splat:\n\nBUG: KASAN: slab-out-of-bounds in memcmp+0x176/0x1d0 lib/string.c:683\nRead of size 8 at addr ffff88810130f5c0 by task syz.1.62/2258\n\nCPU: 2 UID: 0 PID: 2258 Comm: syz.1.62 Not tainted 6.19.0-dirty #3 PREEMPT(voluntary)\nHardware name: QEMU Standard PC (Q35 + ICH9, 2009), BIOS rel-1.17.0-0-gb52ca86e094d-prebuilt.qemu.org 04/01/2014\nCall Trace:\n \u003cTASK\u003e\n __dump_stack lib/dump_stack.c:94 [inline]\n dump_stack_lvl+0xab/0xe0 lib/dump_stack.c:120\n print_address_description mm/kasan/report.c:378 [inline]\n print_report+0xce/0x650 mm/kasan/report.c:482\n kasan_report+0xce/0x100 mm/kasan/report.c:595\n memcmp+0x176/0x1d0 lib/string.c:683\n uuid_equal include/linux/uuid.h:73 [inline]\n cxl_payload_from_user_allowed drivers/cxl/core/mbox.c:345 [inline]\n cxl_mbox_cmd_ctor drivers/cxl/core/mbox.c:368 [inline]\n cxl_validate_cmd_from_user drivers/cxl/core/mbox.c:522 [inline]\n cxl_send_cmd+0x9c0/0xb50 drivers/cxl/core/mbox.c:643\n __cxl_memdev_ioctl drivers/cxl/core/memdev.c:698 [inline]\n cxl_memdev_ioctl+0x14f/0x190 drivers/cxl/core/memdev.c:713\n vfs_ioctl fs/ioctl.c:51 [inline]\n __do_sys_ioctl fs/ioctl.c:597 [inline]\n __se_sys_ioctl fs/ioctl.c:583 [inline]\n __x64_sys_ioctl+0x18e/0x210 fs/ioctl.c:583\n do_syscall_x64 arch/x86/entry/syscall_64.c:63 [inline]\n do_syscall_64+0xa8/0x330 arch/x86/entry/syscall_64.c:94\n entry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x77/0x7f\nRIP: 0033:0x7fdaf331ba79\nCode: ff ff c3 66 2e 0f 1f 84 00 00 00 00 00 0f 1f 40 00 48 89 f8 48 89 f7 48 89 d6 48 89 ca 4d 89 c2 4d 89 c8 4c 8b 4c 24 08 0f 05 \u003c48\u003e 3d 01 f0 ff ff 73 01 c3 48 c7 c1 a8 ff ff ff f7 d8 64 89 01 48\nRSP: 002b:00007fdaf1d77038 EFLAGS: 00000246 ORIG_RAX: 0000000000000010\nRAX: ffffffffffffffda RBX: 00007fdaf3585fa0 RCX: 00007fdaf331ba79\nRDX: 00002000000001c0 RSI: 00000000c030ce02 RDI: 0000000000000003\nRBP: 00007fdaf33749df R08: 0000000000000000 R09: 0000000000000000\nR10: 0000000000000000 R11: 0000000000000246 R12: 0000000000000000\nR13: 00007fdaf3586038 R14: 00007fdaf3585fa0 R15: 00007ffced2af768\n \u003c/TASK\u003e\n\nAdd 'in_size' parameter to cxl_payload_from_user_allowed() and validate\nthe payload is large enough."
              },
