            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-74729",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: soc: aspeed: lpc-snoop: Fix usercopy overflow in snoop_file_read"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nsoc: aspeed: lpc-snoop: Fix usercopy overflow in snoop_file_read\n\nput_fifo_with_discard() acts as both producer and consumer on the kfifo:\nit calls kfifo_skip() (advances out) and kfifo_put() (advances in) from\nthe IRQ handler without synchronizing with snoop_file_read(), which also\nconsumes via kfifo_to_user(). On SMP systems this concurrent access can\nleave (in - out) larger than the ring buffer, so __kfifo_to_user()'s clamp\nto (in - out) is ineffective and kfifo_copy_to_user() can attempt a\ncopy_to_user() past the kmalloc-2k backing store:\n\n  usercopy: Kernel memory exposure attempt detected from SLUB object\n  'kmalloc-2k' (offset 0, size 2049)!\n  kernel BUG at mm/usercopy.c!\n  Call trace:\n   usercopy_abort\n   __check_heap_object\n   __check_object_size\n   kfifo_copy_to_user\n   __kfifo_to_user\n   snoop_file_read\n   vfs_read\n\nSerialize kfifo access with a per-channel spinlock shared between the\nIRQ handler (producer) and the file reader (consumer).  Annotate @fifo\nwith __guarded_by(\u0026lock) and opt the driver into context analysis so the\ncompiler enforces that all fifo access holds the lock."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-74729",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-74729\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-74729](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-74729)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nsoc: aspeed: lpc-snoop: Fix usercopy overflow in snoop_file_read\n\nput_fifo_with_discard() acts as both producer and consumer on the kfifo:\nit calls kfifo_skip() (advances out) and kfifo_put() (advances in) from\nthe IRQ handler without synchronizing with snoop_file_read(), which also\nconsumes via kfifo_to_user(). On SMP systems this concurrent access can\nleave (in - out) larger than the ring buffer, so __kfifo_to_user()'s clamp\nto (in - out) is ineffective and kfifo_copy_to_user() can attempt a\ncopy_to_user() past the kmalloc-2k backing store:\n\n  usercopy: Kernel memory exposure attempt detected from SLUB object\n  'kmalloc-2k' (offset 0, size 2049)!\n  kernel BUG at mm/usercopy.c!\n  Call trace:\n   usercopy_abort\n   __check_heap_object\n   __check_object_size\n   kfifo_copy_to_user\n   __kfifo_to_user\n   snoop_file_read\n   vfs_read\n\nSerialize kfifo access with a per-channel spinlock shared between the\nIRQ handler (producer) and the file reader (consumer).  Annotate @fifo\nwith __guarded_by(\u0026lock) and opt the driver into context analysis so the\ncompiler enforces that all fifo access holds the lock.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-74729**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-74729](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-74729)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nsoc: aspeed: lpc-snoop: Fix usercopy overflow in snoop_file_read\n\nput_fifo_with_discard() acts as both producer and consumer on the kfifo:\nit calls kfifo_skip() (advances out) and kfifo_put() (advances in) from\nthe IRQ handler without synchronizing with snoop_file_read(), which also\nconsumes via kfifo_to_user(). On SMP systems this concurrent access can\nleave (in - out) larger than the ring buffer, so __kfifo_to_user()'s clamp\nto (in - out) is ineffective and kfifo_copy_to_user() can attempt a\ncopy_to_user() past the kmalloc-2k backing store:\n\n  usercopy: Kernel memory exposure attempt detected from SLUB object\n  'kmalloc-2k' (offset 0, size 2049)!\n  kernel BUG at mm/usercopy.c!\n  Call trace:\n   usercopy_abort\n   __check_heap_object\n   __check_object_size\n   kfifo_copy_to_user\n   __kfifo_to_user\n   snoop_file_read\n   vfs_read\n\nSerialize kfifo access with a per-channel spinlock shared between the\nIRQ handler (producer) and the file reader (consumer).  Annotate @fifo\nwith __guarded_by(\u0026lock) and opt the driver into context analysis so the\ncompiler enforces that all fifo access holds the lock."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-74733",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: gpio: pca953x: fix pca953x_irq_bus_sync_unlock regmap lock"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ngpio: pca953x: fix pca953x_irq_bus_sync_unlock regmap lock\n\nLocking is disabled in the regmap config as this driver uses its own\nlock. This means that all calls to regmap functions (read or write) must\nhold the i2c_lock. The function pca953x_irq_bus_sync_unlock() did not do\nthis, and it was therefore possible that multiple threads could cause an\nincorrect register to be read/written.\n\nA previous patch partly fixed this, but only protected the write to the\ninterrupt mask register, and not the read from the direction register."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-74733",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-74733\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-74733](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-74733)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ngpio: pca953x: fix pca953x_irq_bus_sync_unlock regmap lock\n\nLocking is disabled in the regmap config as this driver uses its own\nlock. This means that all calls to regmap functions (read or write) must\nhold the i2c_lock. The function pca953x_irq_bus_sync_unlock() did not do\nthis, and it was therefore possible that multiple threads could cause an\nincorrect register to be read/written.\n\nA previous patch partly fixed this, but only protected the write to the\ninterrupt mask register, and not the read from the direction register.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-74733**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-74733](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-74733)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ngpio: pca953x: fix pca953x_irq_bus_sync_unlock regmap lock\n\nLocking is disabled in the regmap config as this driver uses its own\nlock. This means that all calls to regmap functions (read or write) must\nhold the i2c_lock. The function pca953x_irq_bus_sync_unlock() did not do\nthis, and it was therefore possible that multiple threads could cause an\nincorrect register to be read/written.\n\nA previous patch partly fixed this, but only protected the write to the\ninterrupt mask register, and not the read from the direction register."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-74754",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: scsi: core: pair EH runtime PM get and put"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nscsi: core: pair EH runtime PM get and put\n\nshost-\u003eeh_noresume is currently consulted twice in one error handling\niteration: once before scsi_autopm_get_host() and once again before\nscsi_autopm_put_host().\n\nThat is racy when a PM-triggered error path flips shost-\u003eeh_noresume\nwhile the SCSI EH thread is still running.\n\nThe problem flow looks like this:\nPM path\n  ufshcd_set_dev_pwr_mode()\n    shost-\u003eeh_noresume = 1\n    ufshcd_execute_start_stop  \u003c-- trigger EH\n    ...\n    shost-\u003eeh_noresume = 0\n\nEH path\n  scsi_error_handler()\n    if (!shost-\u003eeh_noresume)\n      scsi_autopm_get_host()  \u003c-- skipped\n    ...\n    if (!shost-\u003eeh_noresume)\n       scsi_autopm_put_host()  \u003c-- executed later\n\nIn that case one EH iteration can skip autoresume on entry and still\ndrop a runtime PM reference on exit. That leaves an unmatched runtime PM\nput and can trigger a runtime PM usage count underflow.\n\nFix this by making eh_noresume a regular bool so it can be accessed with\nREAD_ONCE() and WRITE_ONCE(). Snapshot it once per EH iteration and use\nthat snapshot for both runtime PM get and put decisions."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-74754",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-74754\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-74754](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-74754)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nscsi: core: pair EH runtime PM get and put\n\nshost-\u003eeh_noresume is currently consulted twice in one error handling\niteration: once before scsi_autopm_get_host() and once again before\nscsi_autopm_put_host().\n\nThat is racy when a PM-triggered error path flips shost-\u003eeh_noresume\nwhile the SCSI EH thread is still running.\n\nThe problem flow looks like this:\nPM path\n  ufshcd_set_dev_pwr_mode()\n    shost-\u003eeh_noresume = 1\n    ufshcd_execute_start_stop  \u003c-- trigger EH\n    ...\n    shost-\u003eeh_noresume = 0\n\nEH path\n  scsi_error_handler()\n    if (!shost-\u003eeh_noresume)\n      scsi_autopm_get_host()  \u003c-- skipped\n    ...\n    if (!shost-\u003eeh_noresume)\n       scsi_autopm_put_host()  \u003c-- executed later\n\nIn that case one EH iteration can skip autoresume on entry and still\ndrop a runtime PM reference on exit. That leaves an unmatched runtime PM\nput and can trigger a runtime PM usage count underflow.\n\nFix this by making eh_noresume a regular bool so it can be accessed with\nREAD_ONCE() and WRITE_ONCE(). Snapshot it once per EH iteration and use\nthat snapshot for both runtime PM get and put decisions.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-74754**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-74754](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-74754)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nscsi: core: pair EH runtime PM get and put\n\nshost-\u003eeh_noresume is currently consulted twice in one error handling\niteration: once before scsi_autopm_get_host() and once again before\nscsi_autopm_put_host().\n\nThat is racy when a PM-triggered error path flips shost-\u003eeh_noresume\nwhile the SCSI EH thread is still running.\n\nThe problem flow looks like this:\nPM path\n  ufshcd_set_dev_pwr_mode()\n    shost-\u003eeh_noresume = 1\n    ufshcd_execute_start_stop  \u003c-- trigger EH\n    ...\n    shost-\u003eeh_noresume = 0\n\nEH path\n  scsi_error_handler()\n    if (!shost-\u003eeh_noresume)\n      scsi_autopm_get_host()  \u003c-- skipped\n    ...\n    if (!shost-\u003eeh_noresume)\n       scsi_autopm_put_host()  \u003c-- executed later\n\nIn that case one EH iteration can skip autoresume on entry and still\ndrop a runtime PM reference on exit. That leaves an unmatched runtime PM\nput and can trigger a runtime PM usage count underflow.\n\nFix this by making eh_noresume a regular bool so it can be accessed with\nREAD_ONCE() and WRITE_ONCE(). Snapshot it once per EH iteration and use\nthat snapshot for both runtime PM get and put decisions."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-80556",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: mmc: atmel-mci: Fix use-after-free in atmci_remove due to race condition"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmmc: atmel-mci: Fix use-after-free in atmci_remove due to race condition\n\nIn atmci_probe, \u0026host-\u003ebh_work is bound with atmci_work_func, and\natmci_interrupt, atmci_timeout_timer and atmci_dma_complete can all\nqueue this work on system_bh_wq.\n\nIf we remove the module, atmci_remove makes cleanup and the memory\nallocated for host with devm_kzalloc() is released after the remove\ncallback returns, while the work mentioned above may still be pending\nor running. The sequence of operations that may lead to a UAF bug is\nas follows:\n\nCPU0                                      CPU1\n\n                                          | atmci_interrupt\n                                          | queue_work(system_bh_wq,\n                                          |            \u0026host-\u003ebh_work)\natmci_remove                              |\natmci_cleanup_slot(...)                   |\natmci_writel(host, ATMCI_IDR, ~0UL)       |\ntimer_delete_sync(\u0026host-\u003etimer)           |\ndma_release_channel(host-\u003edma.chan)       |\nfree_irq(platform_get_irq(pdev, 0), host) |\n                                          | atmci_work_func\n                                          | // use host\n// devm resources released after          |\n// remove returns, host is freed          |\n                                          | // use host (use-after-free)\n\nFix it by canceling the work after all the sources that can schedule\nit (IRQ handler, timeout timer and DMA completion callback) have been\nstopped, and before proceeding with the remaining cleanup in\natmci_remove."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80556",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-80556\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-80556](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80556)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmmc: atmel-mci: Fix use-after-free in atmci_remove due to race condition\n\nIn atmci_probe, \u0026host-\u003ebh_work is bound with atmci_work_func, and\natmci_interrupt, atmci_timeout_timer and atmci_dma_complete can all\nqueue this work on system_bh_wq.\n\nIf we remove the module, atmci_remove makes cleanup and the memory\nallocated for host with devm_kzalloc() is released after the remove\ncallback returns, while the work mentioned above may still be pending\nor running. The sequence of operations that may lead to a UAF bug is\nas follows:\n\nCPU0                                      CPU1\n\n                                          | atmci_interrupt\n                                          | queue_work(system_bh_wq,\n                                          |            \u0026host-\u003ebh_work)\natmci_remove                              |\natmci_cleanup_slot(...)                   |\natmci_writel(host, ATMCI_IDR, ~0UL)       |\ntimer_delete_sync(\u0026host-\u003etimer)           |\ndma_release_channel(host-\u003edma.chan)       |\nfree_irq(platform_get_irq(pdev, 0), host) |\n                                          | atmci_work_func\n                                          | // use host\n// devm resources released after          |\n// remove returns, host is freed          |\n                                          | // use host (use-after-free)\n\nFix it by canceling the work after all the sources that can schedule\nit (IRQ handler, timeout timer and DMA completion callback) have been\nstopped, and before proceeding with the remaining cleanup in\natmci_remove.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-80556**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-80556](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80556)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmmc: atmel-mci: Fix use-after-free in atmci_remove due to race condition\n\nIn atmci_probe, \u0026host-\u003ebh_work is bound with atmci_work_func, and\natmci_interrupt, atmci_timeout_timer and atmci_dma_complete can all\nqueue this work on system_bh_wq.\n\nIf we remove the module, atmci_remove makes cleanup and the memory\nallocated for host with devm_kzalloc() is released after the remove\ncallback returns, while the work mentioned above may still be pending\nor running. The sequence of operations that may lead to a UAF bug is\nas follows:\n\nCPU0                                      CPU1\n\n                                          | atmci_interrupt\n                                          | queue_work(system_bh_wq,\n                                          |            \u0026host-\u003ebh_work)\natmci_remove                              |\natmci_cleanup_slot(...)                   |\natmci_writel(host, ATMCI_IDR, ~0UL)       |\ntimer_delete_sync(\u0026host-\u003etimer)           |\ndma_release_channel(host-\u003edma.chan)       |\nfree_irq(platform_get_irq(pdev, 0), host) |\n                                          | atmci_work_func\n                                          | // use host\n// devm resources released after          |\n// remove returns, host is freed          |\n                                          | // use host (use-after-free)\n\nFix it by canceling the work after all the sources that can schedule\nit (IRQ handler, timeout timer and DMA completion callback) have been\nstopped, and before proceeding with the remaining cleanup in\natmci_remove."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-80572",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: Input: byd - synchronize timer deletion before freeing private data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nInput: byd - synchronize timer deletion before freeing private data\n\nbyd_disconnect() uses timer_delete() before freeing the driver's private\ndata.  This does not wait for a running byd_clear_touch() callback, which\ndereferences the private data and its psmouse pointer.  A callback racing\nwith disconnect can therefore access the private data after it has been\nfreed.  The timer can also still be re-armed by byd_process_byte() while\nthe disconnect is in progress.\n\nUse timer_shutdown_sync() before freeing the private data: it waits for\na running callback and turns any later re-arm attempt into a no-op."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80572",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-80572\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-80572](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80572)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nInput: byd - synchronize timer deletion before freeing private data\n\nbyd_disconnect() uses timer_delete() before freeing the driver's private\ndata.  This does not wait for a running byd_clear_touch() callback, which\ndereferences the private data and its psmouse pointer.  A callback racing\nwith disconnect can therefore access the private data after it has been\nfreed.  The timer can also still be re-armed by byd_process_byte() while\nthe disconnect is in progress.\n\nUse timer_shutdown_sync() before freeing the private data: it waits for\na running callback and turns any later re-arm attempt into a no-op.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-80572**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-80572](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80572)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nInput: byd - synchronize timer deletion before freeing private data\n\nbyd_disconnect() uses timer_delete() before freeing the driver's private\ndata.  This does not wait for a running byd_clear_touch() callback, which\ndereferences the private data and its psmouse pointer.  A callback racing\nwith disconnect can therefore access the private data after it has been\nfreed.  The timer can also still be re-armed by byd_process_byte() while\nthe disconnect is in progress.\n\nUse timer_shutdown_sync() before freeing the private data: it waits for\na running callback and turns any later re-arm attempt into a no-op."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-80579",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: fbdev: clear fb_info-\u003emode before deleting a videomode"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nfbdev: clear fb_info-\u003emode before deleting a videomode\n\nfb_set_var() can delete a mode from info-\u003emodelist when userspace\npasses FB_ACTIVATE_INV_MODE through FBIOPUT_VSCREENINFO. The code\nchecks that the mode being deleted is not the current info-\u003evar and\nthat fbcon is not using it, but it does not check fb_info-\u003emode.\n\nfb_info-\u003emode may still point into the modelist entry being deleted.\nIf the entry is freed, later mode sysfs reads through show_mode() can\ndereference a stale pointer.\n\nClear fb_info-\u003emode before calling fb_delete_videomode() when it\nmatches the mode being removed."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80579",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-80579\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-80579](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80579)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nfbdev: clear fb_info-\u003emode before deleting a videomode\n\nfb_set_var() can delete a mode from info-\u003emodelist when userspace\npasses FB_ACTIVATE_INV_MODE through FBIOPUT_VSCREENINFO. The code\nchecks that the mode being deleted is not the current info-\u003evar and\nthat fbcon is not using it, but it does not check fb_info-\u003emode.\n\nfb_info-\u003emode may still point into the modelist entry being deleted.\nIf the entry is freed, later mode sysfs reads through show_mode() can\ndereference a stale pointer.\n\nClear fb_info-\u003emode before calling fb_delete_videomode() when it\nmatches the mode being removed.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-80579**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-80579](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80579)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nfbdev: clear fb_info-\u003emode before deleting a videomode\n\nfb_set_var() can delete a mode from info-\u003emodelist when userspace\npasses FB_ACTIVATE_INV_MODE through FBIOPUT_VSCREENINFO. The code\nchecks that the mode being deleted is not the current info-\u003evar and\nthat fbcon is not using it, but it does not check fb_info-\u003emode.\n\nfb_info-\u003emode may still point into the modelist entry being deleted.\nIf the entry is freed, later mode sysfs reads through show_mode() can\ndereference a stale pointer.\n\nClear fb_info-\u003emode before calling fb_delete_videomode() when it\nmatches the mode being removed."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-80580",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: fbdev: bound mode sysfs output to the sysfs buffer"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nfbdev: bound mode sysfs output to the sysfs buffer\n\nmode_string() uses snprintf() which can return a value larger than the\nremaining buffer space. show_modes() accumulates the return value into i\nwithout checking whether i has reached PAGE_SIZE, causing the offset to\nadvance past the sysfs buffer if the modelist is long enough.\n\nAdd a size parameter to mode_string() and use scnprintf() to return\nonly the bytes actually written. Add an early return when offset\nalready exceeds the buffer. In show_modes(), stop accumulating once\nthe buffer is full."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80580",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-80580\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-80580](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80580)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nfbdev: bound mode sysfs output to the sysfs buffer\n\nmode_string() uses snprintf() which can return a value larger than the\nremaining buffer space. show_modes() accumulates the return value into i\nwithout checking whether i has reached PAGE_SIZE, causing the offset to\nadvance past the sysfs buffer if the modelist is long enough.\n\nAdd a size parameter to mode_string() and use scnprintf() to return\nonly the bytes actually written. Add an early return when offset\nalready exceeds the buffer. In show_modes(), stop accumulating once\nthe buffer is full.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-80580**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-80580](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80580)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nfbdev: bound mode sysfs output to the sysfs buffer\n\nmode_string() uses snprintf() which can return a value larger than the\nremaining buffer space. show_modes() accumulates the return value into i\nwithout checking whether i has reached PAGE_SIZE, causing the offset to\nadvance past the sysfs buffer if the modelist is long enough.\n\nAdd a size parameter to mode_string() and use scnprintf() to return\nonly the bytes actually written. Add an early return when offset\nalready exceeds the buffer. In show_modes(), stop accumulating once\nthe buffer is full."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-80607",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: tracing/probes: Remove WARN_ON_ONCE from parse_btf_arg"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ntracing/probes: Remove WARN_ON_ONCE from parse_btf_arg\n\nSashiko found that user can cause this WARN_ON_ONCE() easily\nwith adding a kprobe event based on a raw address with BTF\nparameter.\n\nSince this is not an unexpected condition, remove the\nWARN_ON_ONCE()."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80607",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-80607\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-80607](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80607)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ntracing/probes: Remove WARN_ON_ONCE from parse_btf_arg\n\nSashiko found that user can cause this WARN_ON_ONCE() easily\nwith adding a kprobe event based on a raw address with BTF\nparameter.\n\nSince this is not an unexpected condition, remove the\nWARN_ON_ONCE().",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-80607**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-80607](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-80607)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ntracing/probes: Remove WARN_ON_ONCE from parse_btf_arg\n\nSashiko found that user can cause this WARN_ON_ONCE() easily\nwith adding a kprobe event based on a raw address with BTF\nparameter.\n\nSince this is not an unexpected condition, remove the\nWARN_ON_ONCE()."
