            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-97503",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: genirq/proc: Size interrupt directory names for 10-digit interrupt numbers"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ngenirq/proc: Size interrupt directory names for 10-digit interrupt numbers\n\n/proc/irq/\u003cn\u003e/ directory names are built in `char name[10]` buffers\nwith `sprintf(name, \"%u\", irq)`.\n\nTen-digit IRQ numbers already need 11 bytes including the trailing NUL, and\ncurrent sparse-IRQ configurations allow interrupt numbers in that range.\n\nSize the temporary name buffer for the current decimal form and switch\nto bounded formatting when creating or removing the proc entry."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97503",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-97503\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-97503](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97503)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ngenirq/proc: Size interrupt directory names for 10-digit interrupt numbers\n\n/proc/irq/\u003cn\u003e/ directory names are built in `char name[10]` buffers\nwith `sprintf(name, \"%u\", irq)`.\n\nTen-digit IRQ numbers already need 11 bytes including the trailing NUL, and\ncurrent sparse-IRQ configurations allow interrupt numbers in that range.\n\nSize the temporary name buffer for the current decimal form and switch\nto bounded formatting when creating or removing the proc entry.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-97503**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-97503](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97503)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ngenirq/proc: Size interrupt directory names for 10-digit interrupt numbers\n\n/proc/irq/\u003cn\u003e/ directory names are built in `char name[10]` buffers\nwith `sprintf(name, \"%u\", irq)`.\n\nTen-digit IRQ numbers already need 11 bytes including the trailing NUL, and\ncurrent sparse-IRQ configurations allow interrupt numbers in that range.\n\nSize the temporary name buffer for the current decimal form and switch\nto bounded formatting when creating or removing the proc entry."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
