            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-98387",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: s390/crypto: Fix skcipher_walk return code handling in aes_s390"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ns390/crypto: Fix skcipher_walk return code handling in aes_s390\n\nThe return codes from skcipher_walk_virt() were not properly checked\nbefore entering the processing loops in ecb_aes_crypt() and ctr_aes_crypt().\nIf skcipher_walk_virt() fails, the walk structure may be in an undefined\nstate, and attempting to process data could lead to incorrect behavior\nor accessing uninitialized memory.\n\nAdd proper return code checking to ensure correct handling of the walk\ninitialization and walk advance and eventually return to the caller\nwith that return code."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98387",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-98387\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-98387](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98387)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ns390/crypto: Fix skcipher_walk return code handling in aes_s390\n\nThe return codes from skcipher_walk_virt() were not properly checked\nbefore entering the processing loops in ecb_aes_crypt() and ctr_aes_crypt().\nIf skcipher_walk_virt() fails, the walk structure may be in an undefined\nstate, and attempting to process data could lead to incorrect behavior\nor accessing uninitialized memory.\n\nAdd proper return code checking to ensure correct handling of the walk\ninitialization and walk advance and eventually return to the caller\nwith that return code.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-98387**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-98387](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98387)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ns390/crypto: Fix skcipher_walk return code handling in aes_s390\n\nThe return codes from skcipher_walk_virt() were not properly checked\nbefore entering the processing loops in ecb_aes_crypt() and ctr_aes_crypt().\nIf skcipher_walk_virt() fails, the walk structure may be in an undefined\nstate, and attempting to process data could lead to incorrect behavior\nor accessing uninitialized memory.\n\nAdd proper return code checking to ensure correct handling of the walk\ninitialization and walk advance and eventually return to the caller\nwith that return code."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-98399",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: s390/ipl: Fix NULL deref in dump_reipl without re-IPL parm block"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ns390/ipl: Fix NULL deref in dump_reipl without re-IPL parm block\n\nUnlike kdump, which passes the re-IPL parameter block through os_info,\nthe stand-alone dump passes it through the IPL parm block address and\nchecksum in lowcore.\n\nSome IPL types, like HMC FTP boot or QEMU direct kernel boot, might not\nprovide an IPL parameter block. In this case reipl_type_init() selects\nIPL_TYPE_UNKNOWN and reipl_block_actual remains NULL. Nevertheless,\ndump_reipl_run() unconditionally dereferences it when preparing the\nlowcore fields. This may happen to work by chance when address zero\ncontains readable lowcore data. A zero IPL parameter block address is\nthen stored in lowcore, causing the stand-alone dumper to enter disabled\nwait after completing the dump.\n\nExplicitly store a zero IPL parameter block address and checksum when no\nre-IPL parameter block is available. This does not change the behavior:\nthe stand-alone dumper completes the dump and halts, while valid re-IPL\nparameter blocks continue to be handled as before."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98399",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-98399\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-98399](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98399)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ns390/ipl: Fix NULL deref in dump_reipl without re-IPL parm block\n\nUnlike kdump, which passes the re-IPL parameter block through os_info,\nthe stand-alone dump passes it through the IPL parm block address and\nchecksum in lowcore.\n\nSome IPL types, like HMC FTP boot or QEMU direct kernel boot, might not\nprovide an IPL parameter block. In this case reipl_type_init() selects\nIPL_TYPE_UNKNOWN and reipl_block_actual remains NULL. Nevertheless,\ndump_reipl_run() unconditionally dereferences it when preparing the\nlowcore fields. This may happen to work by chance when address zero\ncontains readable lowcore data. A zero IPL parameter block address is\nthen stored in lowcore, causing the stand-alone dumper to enter disabled\nwait after completing the dump.\n\nExplicitly store a zero IPL parameter block address and checksum when no\nre-IPL parameter block is available. This does not change the behavior:\nthe stand-alone dumper completes the dump and halts, while valid re-IPL\nparameter blocks continue to be handled as before.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-98399**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-98399](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98399)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ns390/ipl: Fix NULL deref in dump_reipl without re-IPL parm block\n\nUnlike kdump, which passes the re-IPL parameter block through os_info,\nthe stand-alone dump passes it through the IPL parm block address and\nchecksum in lowcore.\n\nSome IPL types, like HMC FTP boot or QEMU direct kernel boot, might not\nprovide an IPL parameter block. In this case reipl_type_init() selects\nIPL_TYPE_UNKNOWN and reipl_block_actual remains NULL. Nevertheless,\ndump_reipl_run() unconditionally dereferences it when preparing the\nlowcore fields. This may happen to work by chance when address zero\ncontains readable lowcore data. A zero IPL parameter block address is\nthen stored in lowcore, causing the stand-alone dumper to enter disabled\nwait after completing the dump.\n\nExplicitly store a zero IPL parameter block address and checksum when no\nre-IPL parameter block is available. This does not change the behavior:\nthe stand-alone dumper completes the dump and halts, while valid re-IPL\nparameter blocks continue to be handled as before."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-98400",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: s390/ipl: Fix NULL deref in kdump without re-IPL parm block"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ns390/ipl: Fix NULL deref in kdump without re-IPL parm block\n\nSome IPL types, like HMC FTP boot or QEMU direct kernel boot, might\nnot provide an IPL parameter block. In this case, reipl_type_init()\nselects IPL_TYPE_UNKNOWN, and reipl_block_actual remains NULL.\n\nkdump passes the re-IPL parameter block to the dump kernel through\nos_info. Before commit 3b9678472bab (\"s390/ipl: correct kdump reipl\nblock checksum calculation\"), the os_info entry was added only for\nIPL types which initialized reipl_block_actual. That commit moved the\nos_info update to machine_crash_shutdown(), making it unconditional. As\na result, set_os_info_reipl_block() dereferences reipl_block_actual for\nIPL_TYPE_UNKNOWN. This may happen to work by chance when address zero\ncontains readable lowcore data and the resulting empty os_info entry is\nignored by the dump kernel.\n\nSkip the os_info update when no re-IPL parameter block is available.\nKdump then collect the dump and reboot without setting re-IPL parameter\nblock."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98400",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-98400\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-98400](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98400)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ns390/ipl: Fix NULL deref in kdump without re-IPL parm block\n\nSome IPL types, like HMC FTP boot or QEMU direct kernel boot, might\nnot provide an IPL parameter block. In this case, reipl_type_init()\nselects IPL_TYPE_UNKNOWN, and reipl_block_actual remains NULL.\n\nkdump passes the re-IPL parameter block to the dump kernel through\nos_info. Before commit 3b9678472bab (\"s390/ipl: correct kdump reipl\nblock checksum calculation\"), the os_info entry was added only for\nIPL types which initialized reipl_block_actual. That commit moved the\nos_info update to machine_crash_shutdown(), making it unconditional. As\na result, set_os_info_reipl_block() dereferences reipl_block_actual for\nIPL_TYPE_UNKNOWN. This may happen to work by chance when address zero\ncontains readable lowcore data and the resulting empty os_info entry is\nignored by the dump kernel.\n\nSkip the os_info update when no re-IPL parameter block is available.\nKdump then collect the dump and reboot without setting re-IPL parameter\nblock.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-98400**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-98400](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98400)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ns390/ipl: Fix NULL deref in kdump without re-IPL parm block\n\nSome IPL types, like HMC FTP boot or QEMU direct kernel boot, might\nnot provide an IPL parameter block. In this case, reipl_type_init()\nselects IPL_TYPE_UNKNOWN, and reipl_block_actual remains NULL.\n\nkdump passes the re-IPL parameter block to the dump kernel through\nos_info. Before commit 3b9678472bab (\"s390/ipl: correct kdump reipl\nblock checksum calculation\"), the os_info entry was added only for\nIPL types which initialized reipl_block_actual. That commit moved the\nos_info update to machine_crash_shutdown(), making it unconditional. As\na result, set_os_info_reipl_block() dereferences reipl_block_actual for\nIPL_TYPE_UNKNOWN. This may happen to work by chance when address zero\ncontains readable lowcore data and the resulting empty os_info entry is\nignored by the dump kernel.\n\nSkip the os_info update when no re-IPL parameter block is available.\nKdump then collect the dump and reboot without setting re-IPL parameter\nblock."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-98403",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: s390/boot: Avoid IPL parameter append past command line"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ns390/boot: Avoid IPL parameter append past command line\n\nA command line may occupy all but the terminating byte of\nCOMMAND_LINE_SIZE. In that case append_ipl_block_parm() passes a zero size\nto the IPL parameter conversion helpers and points the destination one\nbyte past early_command_line. The helpers subtract one from the unsigned\nsize and write the converted parameter outside the command line buffer.\n\nConvert the IPL parameter in the command line parsing buffer first. A\nparameter beginning with '=' can then replace the existing command line\nregardless of its length, while other parameters are appended only when\nspace remains."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98403",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-98403\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-98403](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98403)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ns390/boot: Avoid IPL parameter append past command line\n\nA command line may occupy all but the terminating byte of\nCOMMAND_LINE_SIZE. In that case append_ipl_block_parm() passes a zero size\nto the IPL parameter conversion helpers and points the destination one\nbyte past early_command_line. The helpers subtract one from the unsigned\nsize and write the converted parameter outside the command line buffer.\n\nConvert the IPL parameter in the command line parsing buffer first. A\nparameter beginning with '=' can then replace the existing command line\nregardless of its length, while other parameters are appended only when\nspace remains.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-98403**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-98403](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98403)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ns390/boot: Avoid IPL parameter append past command line\n\nA command line may occupy all but the terminating byte of\nCOMMAND_LINE_SIZE. In that case append_ipl_block_parm() passes a zero size\nto the IPL parameter conversion helpers and points the destination one\nbyte past early_command_line. The helpers subtract one from the unsigned\nsize and write the converted parameter outside the command line buffer.\n\nConvert the IPL parameter in the command line parsing buffer first. A\nparameter beginning with '=' can then replace the existing command line\nregardless of its length, while other parameters are appended only when\nspace remains."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
