            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-98423",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: wifi: mac80211_hwsim: don't hand frames to mac80211 while stopping"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: mac80211_hwsim: don't hand frames to mac80211 while stopping\n\nThe code checks -\u003estarted for frames coming from wmediumd, but the\nradio can be stopped after the check and before frame delivery,\ncausing mac80211 to hit the WARN_ON(!local-\u003estarted).\n\nExpand the mutex for this case and synchronise against it when the\nradio is stopped to avoid being able to hit the warning with hwsim.\n\nDrop the error print that would've complicated the error path, it\nonly triggers for allocation failures (already noisy) and malformed\nframes anyway."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98423",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-98423\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-98423](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98423)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: mac80211_hwsim: don't hand frames to mac80211 while stopping\n\nThe code checks -\u003estarted for frames coming from wmediumd, but the\nradio can be stopped after the check and before frame delivery,\ncausing mac80211 to hit the WARN_ON(!local-\u003estarted).\n\nExpand the mutex for this case and synchronise against it when the\nradio is stopped to avoid being able to hit the warning with hwsim.\n\nDrop the error print that would've complicated the error path, it\nonly triggers for allocation failures (already noisy) and malformed\nframes anyway.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-98423**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-98423](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98423)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: mac80211_hwsim: don't hand frames to mac80211 while stopping\n\nThe code checks -\u003estarted for frames coming from wmediumd, but the\nradio can be stopped after the check and before frame delivery,\ncausing mac80211 to hit the WARN_ON(!local-\u003estarted).\n\nExpand the mutex for this case and synchronise against it when the\nradio is stopped to avoid being able to hit the warning with hwsim.\n\nDrop the error print that would've complicated the error path, it\nonly triggers for allocation failures (already noisy) and malformed\nframes anyway."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-98424",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: wifi: cfg80211: undo netns switch if renaming the wiphy fails"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: cfg80211: undo netns switch if renaming the wiphy fails\n\nOnce all the interfaces have been moved, cfg80211_switch_netns()\nmoves the wiphy itself by setting its network namespace and then\nrenaming it, which makes sysfs move it. The rename can fail (but\nonly on allocation failures), leaving things mixed up and hitting\nthe warning there.\n\nIgnoring it isn't great, undo the move and let the change fail\nin this case. If undo fails then WARN, then things would again\nbe stuck in two different network namespaces."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98424",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-98424\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-98424](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98424)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: cfg80211: undo netns switch if renaming the wiphy fails\n\nOnce all the interfaces have been moved, cfg80211_switch_netns()\nmoves the wiphy itself by setting its network namespace and then\nrenaming it, which makes sysfs move it. The rename can fail (but\nonly on allocation failures), leaving things mixed up and hitting\nthe warning there.\n\nIgnoring it isn't great, undo the move and let the change fail\nin this case. If undo fails then WARN, then things would again\nbe stuck in two different network namespaces.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-98424**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-98424](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98424)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: cfg80211: undo netns switch if renaming the wiphy fails\n\nOnce all the interfaces have been moved, cfg80211_switch_netns()\nmoves the wiphy itself by setting its network namespace and then\nrenaming it, which makes sysfs move it. The rename can fail (but\nonly on allocation failures), leaving things mixed up and hitting\nthe warning there.\n\nIgnoring it isn't great, undo the move and let the change fail\nin this case. If undo fails then WARN, then things would again\nbe stuck in two different network namespaces."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-98425",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: wifi: mac80211: don't warn when an IBSS has no channel to scan"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: mac80211: don't warn when an IBSS has no channel to scan\n\nieee80211_request_ibss_scan() warns when regulatory leaves no\nallowed channel, but that can happen as the regdomain can change\nwhile IBSS is operating, and it can continue to operate briefly\nduring the 60s grace period until it's shut down.\n\nJust remove the warning in this case."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98425",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-98425\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-98425](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98425)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: mac80211: don't warn when an IBSS has no channel to scan\n\nieee80211_request_ibss_scan() warns when regulatory leaves no\nallowed channel, but that can happen as the regdomain can change\nwhile IBSS is operating, and it can continue to operate briefly\nduring the 60s grace period until it's shut down.\n\nJust remove the warning in this case.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-98425**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-98425](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98425)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: mac80211: don't warn when an IBSS has no channel to scan\n\nieee80211_request_ibss_scan() warns when regulatory leaves no\nallowed channel, but that can happen as the regdomain can change\nwhile IBSS is operating, and it can continue to operate briefly\nduring the 60s grace period until it's shut down.\n\nJust remove the warning in this case."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-98430",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: RDMA/efa: Keep admin queues alive while IRQ is registered"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nRDMA/efa: Keep admin queues alive while IRQ is registered\n\nThe management IRQ handler accesses both the admin completion queue and the\nasync event queue. The driver registered the IRQ before constructing these\nqueues and destroyed them before freeing the IRQ, so the handler's lifetime\nwas not contained by the resources it accesses.\n\nInitialize the queues with interrupts masked, request the IRQ, and then\nswitch to interrupt mode. On removal, reset the device and free the IRQ\nbefore destroying the queues. Also reset the device before destroying the\nqueues if IRQ registration fails, because the device already has their DMA\naddresses."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98430",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-98430\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-98430](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98430)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nRDMA/efa: Keep admin queues alive while IRQ is registered\n\nThe management IRQ handler accesses both the admin completion queue and the\nasync event queue. The driver registered the IRQ before constructing these\nqueues and destroyed them before freeing the IRQ, so the handler's lifetime\nwas not contained by the resources it accesses.\n\nInitialize the queues with interrupts masked, request the IRQ, and then\nswitch to interrupt mode. On removal, reset the device and free the IRQ\nbefore destroying the queues. Also reset the device before destroying the\nqueues if IRQ registration fails, because the device already has their DMA\naddresses.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-98430**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-98430](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98430)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nRDMA/efa: Keep admin queues alive while IRQ is registered\n\nThe management IRQ handler accesses both the admin completion queue and the\nasync event queue. The driver registered the IRQ before constructing these\nqueues and destroyed them before freeing the IRQ, so the handler's lifetime\nwas not contained by the resources it accesses.\n\nInitialize the queues with interrupts masked, request the IRQ, and then\nswitch to interrupt mode. On removal, reset the device and free the IRQ\nbefore destroying the queues. Also reset the device before destroying the\nqueues if IRQ registration fails, because the device already has their DMA\naddresses."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-98431",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "kernel: RDMA/uverbs: Fix mmap_lock/disassociation_lock circular dependency"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nRDMA/uverbs: Fix mmap_lock/disassociation_lock circular dependency\n\nCommit 51976c6cd786 (\"RDMA/core: Provide rdma_user_mmap_disassociate()\nto disassociate mmap pages\") introduced disassociation_lock to protect\nnew mmap registrations against uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate(), but\ncreated an ABBA deadlock:\n\n  Thread A (mmap / fork):\n    mmap_lock -\u003e disassociation_lock\n\n  Thread B (disassociate):\n    disassociation_lock -\u003e mmap_lock\n\nFix by removing disassociation_lock entirely and using the pre-existing\nhw_destroy_rwsem instead. hw_destroy_rwsem already provides the same\nprotection: rdma_umap_open() and ib_uverbs_mmap() both use\ndown_read_trylock() before registering a new VMA, so holding hw_destroy_rwsem\nin uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate() is sufficient to block new registrations.\ntrylock is used in both mmap paths (not blocking down_read) because\nmmap_lock is already held on entry, and uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate()\nacquires mmap_lock internally — a blocking read would recreate the same\ndeadlock.\n\nThe only caller that was not taking hw_destroy_rwsem for write was\nrdma_user_mmap_disassociate(). Fix it to take the rwsem per-ufile while\niterating under lists_mutex.  This is safe because ib_uverbs_close()\nreleases hw_destroy_rwsem entirely before acquiring lists_mutex, so the\ntwo locks are never held simultaneously.\n\nlockdep warning:\n\n [  776.654252] ======================================================\n [  776.655214] WARNING: possible circular locking dependency detected\n [  776.656167] 6.18.0for-upstream_debug_94e244d9ccab #1 Not tainted\n [  776.657114] ------------------------------------------------------\n [  776.658087] devlink/14824 is trying to acquire lock:\n [  776.658879] ffff88811170c800 (\u0026mm-\u003emmap_lock){++++}-{4:4}, at: uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate+0x168/0x780 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.660479]\n [  776.660479] but task is already holding lock:\n [  776.661460] ffff888142d92b08 (\u0026file-\u003edisassociation_lock){+.+.}-{4:4}, at: uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate+0x39/0x780 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.663177]\n [  776.663177] which lock already depends on the new lock.\n [  776.663177]\n [  776.664525]\n [  776.664525] the existing dependency chain (in reverse order) is:\n [  776.665724]\n [  776.665724] -\u003e #2 (\u0026file-\u003edisassociation_lock){+.+.}-{4:4}:\n [  776.666887]        __mutex_lock+0x16d/0x2330\n [  776.667633]        rdma_umap_open+0x129/0x280 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.668489]        dup_mmap+0xa40/0x1790\n [  776.669170]        copy_process+0x5dd2/0x6170\n [  776.669933]        kernel_clone+0xb6/0x610\n [  776.670636]        __do_sys_clone+0xb5/0xf0\n [  776.671354]        do_syscall_64+0x70/0x12e0\n [  776.672083]        entry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x4b/0x53\n [  776.672940]\n [  776.672940] -\u003e #1 (\u0026mm-\u003emmap_lock/1){+.+.}-{4:4}:\n [  776.673985]        down_write_nested+0x90/0x1e0\n [  776.674751]        dup_mmap+0x201/0x1790\n [  776.675448]        copy_process+0x5dd2/0x6170\n [  776.676180]        kernel_clone+0xb6/0x610\n [  776.676904]        __do_sys_clone+0xb5/0xf0\n [  776.677615]        do_syscall_64+0x70/0x12e0\n [  776.678351]        entry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x4b/0x53\n [  776.679239]\n [  776.679239] -\u003e #0 (\u0026mm-\u003emmap_lock){++++}-{4:4}:\n [  776.680253]        __lock_acquire+0x18c6/0x2ec0\n [  776.681018]        lock_acquire+0x10e/0x2e0\n [  776.681742]        down_read+0x95/0x430\n [  776.682395]        uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate+0x168/0x780 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.683436]        uverbs_destroy_ufile_hw+0x1ae/0x270 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.684416]        ib_uverbs_remove_one+0x22b/0x420 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.685371]        remove_client_context+0xa6/0xf0 [ib_core]\n [  776.686342]        disable_device+0x12b/0x240 [ib_core]\n [  776.687249]        __ib_unregister_device+0x269/0x460 [ib_core]\n [  776.688233]        ib_unregister_device+0x21/0x30 [ib_core]\n [  776.689140]        mlx5r_remove+0xd0/0x170 [mlx5_ib]\n [  776.689999]        device_release_driver_internal+0x3b2/0x5\n---truncated---"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98431",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-98431\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: linux-libc-dev\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-98431](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98431)\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nRDMA/uverbs: Fix mmap_lock/disassociation_lock circular dependency\n\nCommit 51976c6cd786 (\"RDMA/core: Provide rdma_user_mmap_disassociate()\nto disassociate mmap pages\") introduced disassociation_lock to protect\nnew mmap registrations against uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate(), but\ncreated an ABBA deadlock:\n\n  Thread A (mmap / fork):\n    mmap_lock -\u003e disassociation_lock\n\n  Thread B (disassociate):\n    disassociation_lock -\u003e mmap_lock\n\nFix by removing disassociation_lock entirely and using the pre-existing\nhw_destroy_rwsem instead. hw_destroy_rwsem already provides the same\nprotection: rdma_umap_open() and ib_uverbs_mmap() both use\ndown_read_trylock() before registering a new VMA, so holding hw_destroy_rwsem\nin uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate() is sufficient to block new registrations.\ntrylock is used in both mmap paths (not blocking down_read) because\nmmap_lock is already held on entry, and uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate()\nacquires mmap_lock internally — a blocking read would recreate the same\ndeadlock.\n\nThe only caller that was not taking hw_destroy_rwsem for write was\nrdma_user_mmap_disassociate(). Fix it to take the rwsem per-ufile while\niterating under lists_mutex.  This is safe because ib_uverbs_close()\nreleases hw_destroy_rwsem entirely before acquiring lists_mutex, so the\ntwo locks are never held simultaneously.\n\nlockdep warning:\n\n [  776.654252] ======================================================\n [  776.655214] WARNING: possible circular locking dependency detected\n [  776.656167] 6.18.0for-upstream_debug_94e244d9ccab #1 Not tainted\n [  776.657114] ------------------------------------------------------\n [  776.658087] devlink/14824 is trying to acquire lock:\n [  776.658879] ffff88811170c800 (\u0026mm-\u003emmap_lock){++++}-{4:4}, at: uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate+0x168/0x780 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.660479]\n [  776.660479] but task is already holding lock:\n [  776.661460] ffff888142d92b08 (\u0026file-\u003edisassociation_lock){+.+.}-{4:4}, at: uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate+0x39/0x780 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.663177]\n [  776.663177] which lock already depends on the new lock.\n [  776.663177]\n [  776.664525]\n [  776.664525] the existing dependency chain (in reverse order) is:\n [  776.665724]\n [  776.665724] -\u003e #2 (\u0026file-\u003edisassociation_lock){+.+.}-{4:4}:\n [  776.666887]        __mutex_lock+0x16d/0x2330\n [  776.667633]        rdma_umap_open+0x129/0x280 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.668489]        dup_mmap+0xa40/0x1790\n [  776.669170]        copy_process+0x5dd2/0x6170\n [  776.669933]        kernel_clone+0xb6/0x610\n [  776.670636]        __do_sys_clone+0xb5/0xf0\n [  776.671354]        do_syscall_64+0x70/0x12e0\n [  776.672083]        entry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x4b/0x53\n [  776.672940]\n [  776.672940] -\u003e #1 (\u0026mm-\u003emmap_lock/1){+.+.}-{4:4}:\n [  776.673985]        down_write_nested+0x90/0x1e0\n [  776.674751]        dup_mmap+0x201/0x1790\n [  776.675448]        copy_process+0x5dd2/0x6170\n [  776.676180]        kernel_clone+0xb6/0x610\n [  776.676904]        __do_sys_clone+0xb5/0xf0\n [  776.677615]        do_syscall_64+0x70/0x12e0\n [  776.678351]        entry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x4b/0x53\n [  776.679239]\n [  776.679239] -\u003e #0 (\u0026mm-\u003emmap_lock){++++}-{4:4}:\n [  776.680253]        __lock_acquire+0x18c6/0x2ec0\n [  776.681018]        lock_acquire+0x10e/0x2e0\n [  776.681742]        down_read+0x95/0x430\n [  776.682395]        uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate+0x168/0x780 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.683436]        uverbs_destroy_ufile_hw+0x1ae/0x270 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.684416]        ib_uverbs_remove_one+0x22b/0x420 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.685371]        remove_client_context+0xa6/0xf0 [ib_core]\n [  776.686342]        disable_device+0x12b/0x240 [ib_core]\n [  776.687249]        __ib_unregister_device+0x269/0x460 [ib_core]\n [  776.688233]        ib_unregister_device+0x21/0x30 [ib_core]\n [  776.689140]        mlx5r_remove+0xd0/0x170 [mlx5_ib]\n [  776.689999]        device_release_driver_internal+0x3b2/0x5\n---truncated---",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-98431**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|linux-libc-dev||[CVE-2026-98431](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-98431)|\n\nIn the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nRDMA/uverbs: Fix mmap_lock/disassociation_lock circular dependency\n\nCommit 51976c6cd786 (\"RDMA/core: Provide rdma_user_mmap_disassociate()\nto disassociate mmap pages\") introduced disassociation_lock to protect\nnew mmap registrations against uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate(), but\ncreated an ABBA deadlock:\n\n  Thread A (mmap / fork):\n    mmap_lock -\u003e disassociation_lock\n\n  Thread B (disassociate):\n    disassociation_lock -\u003e mmap_lock\n\nFix by removing disassociation_lock entirely and using the pre-existing\nhw_destroy_rwsem instead. hw_destroy_rwsem already provides the same\nprotection: rdma_umap_open() and ib_uverbs_mmap() both use\ndown_read_trylock() before registering a new VMA, so holding hw_destroy_rwsem\nin uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate() is sufficient to block new registrations.\ntrylock is used in both mmap paths (not blocking down_read) because\nmmap_lock is already held on entry, and uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate()\nacquires mmap_lock internally — a blocking read would recreate the same\ndeadlock.\n\nThe only caller that was not taking hw_destroy_rwsem for write was\nrdma_user_mmap_disassociate(). Fix it to take the rwsem per-ufile while\niterating under lists_mutex.  This is safe because ib_uverbs_close()\nreleases hw_destroy_rwsem entirely before acquiring lists_mutex, so the\ntwo locks are never held simultaneously.\n\nlockdep warning:\n\n [  776.654252] ======================================================\n [  776.655214] WARNING: possible circular locking dependency detected\n [  776.656167] 6.18.0for-upstream_debug_94e244d9ccab #1 Not tainted\n [  776.657114] ------------------------------------------------------\n [  776.658087] devlink/14824 is trying to acquire lock:\n [  776.658879] ffff88811170c800 (\u0026mm-\u003emmap_lock){++++}-{4:4}, at: uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate+0x168/0x780 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.660479]\n [  776.660479] but task is already holding lock:\n [  776.661460] ffff888142d92b08 (\u0026file-\u003edisassociation_lock){+.+.}-{4:4}, at: uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate+0x39/0x780 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.663177]\n [  776.663177] which lock already depends on the new lock.\n [  776.663177]\n [  776.664525]\n [  776.664525] the existing dependency chain (in reverse order) is:\n [  776.665724]\n [  776.665724] -\u003e #2 (\u0026file-\u003edisassociation_lock){+.+.}-{4:4}:\n [  776.666887]        __mutex_lock+0x16d/0x2330\n [  776.667633]        rdma_umap_open+0x129/0x280 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.668489]        dup_mmap+0xa40/0x1790\n [  776.669170]        copy_process+0x5dd2/0x6170\n [  776.669933]        kernel_clone+0xb6/0x610\n [  776.670636]        __do_sys_clone+0xb5/0xf0\n [  776.671354]        do_syscall_64+0x70/0x12e0\n [  776.672083]        entry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x4b/0x53\n [  776.672940]\n [  776.672940] -\u003e #1 (\u0026mm-\u003emmap_lock/1){+.+.}-{4:4}:\n [  776.673985]        down_write_nested+0x90/0x1e0\n [  776.674751]        dup_mmap+0x201/0x1790\n [  776.675448]        copy_process+0x5dd2/0x6170\n [  776.676180]        kernel_clone+0xb6/0x610\n [  776.676904]        __do_sys_clone+0xb5/0xf0\n [  776.677615]        do_syscall_64+0x70/0x12e0\n [  776.678351]        entry_SYSCALL_64_after_hwframe+0x4b/0x53\n [  776.679239]\n [  776.679239] -\u003e #0 (\u0026mm-\u003emmap_lock){++++}-{4:4}:\n [  776.680253]        __lock_acquire+0x18c6/0x2ec0\n [  776.681018]        lock_acquire+0x10e/0x2e0\n [  776.681742]        down_read+0x95/0x430\n [  776.682395]        uverbs_user_mmap_disassociate+0x168/0x780 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.683436]        uverbs_destroy_ufile_hw+0x1ae/0x270 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.684416]        ib_uverbs_remove_one+0x22b/0x420 [ib_uverbs]\n [  776.685371]        remove_client_context+0xa6/0xf0 [ib_core]\n [  776.686342]        disable_device+0x12b/0x240 [ib_core]\n [  776.687249]        __ib_unregister_device+0x269/0x460 [ib_core]\n [  776.688233]        ib_unregister_device+0x21/0x30 [ib_core]\n [  776.689140]        mlx5r_remove+0xd0/0x170 [mlx5_ib]\n [  776.689999]        device_release_driver_internal+0x3b2/0x5\n---truncated---"
              },
